{"id": "586066d1-b734-4971-ad31-3c2bb49fb067", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T14:22:10.982007", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "5013c2c8-c631-4884-a66f-0d08020130fd", "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-d3faec0f-2211-4bcf-b931-205ac38d7418", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"phase\": \"exploration\", \"command\": \"echo \\\"=== python ===\\\" && python3 --version 2>&1; echo \\\"=== nproc ===\\\" && nproc 2>&1; echo \\\"=== torch check ===\\\" && python3 -c \\\"import torch; print('torch', torch.__version__, 'cuda', torch.cuda.is_available())\\\" 2>&1 | head; echo \\\"=== gpu ===\\\" && (command -v nvidia-smi && nvidia-smi 2>&1 | head -5) || echo \\\"no nvidia-smi\\\"\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 0.032, \"output\": \"=== python ===\\nPython 3.12.3\\n=== nproc ===\\n24\\n=== torch check ===\\nTraceback (most recent call last):\\n  File \\\"<string>\\\", line 1, in <module>\\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'torch'\\n=== gpu ===\\n/usr/bin/nvidia-smi\\nNVIDIA-SMI has failed because it couldn't communicate with the NVIDIA driver. Make sure that the latest NVIDIA driver is installed and running.\\n\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "bed5d440-24f0-4a7f-8360-0c34f0ac088b", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent", "source_event_index": 13}
{"id": "81e5ba57-be49-489a-9e34-f9ccf5e9f24d", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T14:23:00.361118", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "586066d1-b734-4971-ad31-3c2bb49fb067", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-a52d113c-585e-49f1-9445-2abce8519437", "tool_call": {"id": "call-a52d113c-585e-49f1-9445-2abce8519437", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Initial plan: inspection + sketch + assumptions\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"## (1) Repository inspection — findings\\n\\n**Workspace contents: effectively empty of research material.**\\n\\n- `/workspace` contains a single file: `task.json` (511 bytes). It restates the research question and the contextual note `track.md`: *\\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"* No configuration files, no SiT/Hyena source, no notebooks, no PDFs, no notes.\\n- `/workspace` tree at depth 2: only the root and the `task.json` file.\\n- Other common locations (`$HOME`, `/tmp`, `/data`) are all empty.\\n- No SiT, Hyena, paper, repo, or research directories exist under `/` up to depth 3 (only unrelated system paths such as `/usr/share/maven-repo`, `/lib/gnome-shell/girepository-1.0` were matched; no topical overlap).\\n- Available compute in this sandbox: Python 3.12.3, 24 CPU cores, **no GPU** (`nvidia-smi` reports it cannot communicate with a driver), **no `torch`** installed, network disabled, bubblewrap isolation.\\n\\n**Implication.** There is nothing to draw on locally. Any architectural definitions of SiT's \\\"time attention\\\", Hyena's operator structure (gated convolutions + multiplicative gating / long convolutions), and any prior reported results must come from researcher clarification or from canonical published sources rather than from artefacts already present. The task framing in `task.json` says design-only and explicitly says repositories and compute are not required, which is consistent with the empty workspace.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (2) Proposed approach sketch (concise)\\n\\n### Central question (paraphrased and likely to require your sign-off)\\nWhether swapping SiT's time-axis attention for a **non-causal** Hyena operator (i.e., a bidirectional / long-convolution mixer that explicitly looks both forward and backward along the time axis) lets the model produce **very long** trajectory roll-outs whose short-, medium-, and long-timescale structure all remain consistent.\\n\\n### Two configurations to compare\\n\\n| Arm | Time-axis operator | Causality |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **A — SiT-TimeAttn** (baseline) | SiT's original time attention (causal or whatever the source uses — needs clarification) | as in original SiT |\\n| **B — SiT-Hyena** (treatment) | Hyena-style operator replacing time attention: implicit long convolutions (e.g., two short convs gated by elementwise MLPs and a multiplicative gate), **non-causal** — applied bidirectionally across the time axis | non-causal (bidirectional) |\\n\\nEverything else kept identical: spatial mixing, conditioning pathway, embedding, optimiser, schedule, batch, seed.\\n\\n### Candidate datasets / trajectory types (pick one or two; needs your call)\\n\\n1. **Synthetic multi-timescale sequence** (recommended for clean ablation): a 1-D scalar trajectory with superimposed components at three timescales (e.g., fast oscillation + medium modulation + slow drift), generated from a known SDE/ODE so ground-truth PSD / autocorrelation / phase coherence are available. Best for \\\"multiple timescales\\\" disentanglement.\\n2. **Physical dynamics trajectory**: classic 2-D / 3-D chaotic or near-chaotic dynamical system (e.g., Lorenz-63, double pendulum) — known multiscale structure, well-posed evaluation via forecast skill, Lyapunov-style horizon, power spectrum, distributional divergence.\\n3. **Moving-MNIST / motion sequences** — established trajectory-style benchmark; supports visual qualitative checks and quantitative FID/FVD on long roll-outs.\\n4. **Long-horizon video frames** (e.g., UCF-101 subset, Kinetics subset) — most aligned with \\\"SiT video\\\" lineage, but heavier and noisier for a clean ablation.\\n\\nSequence lengths to span \\\"extremely long\\\" without ambiguity: pick an **evaluation ladder** (e.g., 64 / 256 / 1024 / 4096 steps, or analogous pixel-time horizons for video) so we can plot degradation and locate where Hyena's sub-quadratic cost diverges from attention's.\\n\\n### Candidate primary metrics\\n\\n| Pillar | Metric | Why |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Long-horizon trajectory consistency | (a) rollout forecast skill (RMSE/NLL) vs. analytical truth / held-out future, computed at multiple horizons; (b) **spectral / multi-timescale fidelity**: per-band PSD error, log-power-spectrum MSE, autocorrelation consistency at multiple lags | directly answers \\\"consistent\\\" and \\\"multiple timescales\\\" |\\n| Multi-timescale fidelity | separate per-timescale PSD/MSE after band-pass decomposition; phase coherence; distributional divergence (Wasserstein-1) on returns | isolates where one operator beats the other |\\n| Computational cost | wall-clock per training step; parameters; FLOPs and **activation memory** vs. sequence length | one of the purported benefits of Hyena; required for the matched-cost control |\\n| Training stability | gradient-norm curves, loss curves, NaN/Inf events, effective rank of features | non-causal mixing can change optimisation landscape |\\n\\nA single scalar **primary endpoint** should be picked (e.g., log-PSD error at the longest horizon summed across timescale bands), with others as **secondary/supporting** — needs your call.\\n\\n### Controls (high-level)\\n\\n- **Matched parameters or matched FLOPs** (one of these, not both — likely matched FLOPs for a fair cost comparison, with #params reported separately).\\n- **Matched training schedule**: same optimiser, lr schedule, warm-up, total tokens / steps, batch size, seed, mixed-precision policy.\\n- **Identical conditioning**: same noise schedule (DDPM/Flow-matching), same conditioning inputs (class / time / any extra), same sampling procedure (e.g., same NFE, same solver at eval).\\n- **Identical spatial mixing** and identical everything except the time-axis operator.\\n- **Ablation sub-arms** worth including if budget allows: causal vs. non-causal Hyena (separates \\\"Hyena\\\" vs. \\\"non-causality\\\"); different sequence-length training regimes (train-short / eval-long) to stress long-range generalisation.\\n\\n### Sub-questions the experiment should be able to answer\\n1. Does SiT-Hyena match SiT-TimeAttn in multi-timescale fidelity at short horizons?\\n2. Where does the relative error of each arm cross over as horizon grows?\\n3. Is SiT-Hyena's per-step cost genuinely sub-quadratic and does the gap widen with length?\\n4. Does non-causality cause any pathology at inference (boundary effects from bidirectional mixing at generation time — needs separate handling design)?\\n5. Training stability: comparable or not?\\n\\n### Non-causality handling at inference (flag, will need explicit design)\\nAt generation time, a non-causal operator cannot strictly use future frames. Options: (i) two-pass rollout with a buffer of past+future predictions and a substitution rule; (ii) streaming with a fixed future window; (iii) teacher-forcing-style chunked rollouts. **This choice can dominate the comparison** and must be fixed identically across arms (with non-causal Hyena granted an equivalent adaptation if applied to attention too) — needs your call.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (3) Assumptions and uncertainties\\n\\n### Consequential assumptions I would need to make to finalise the design\\n\\n| # | Assumption | Why it matters | Resolvable only by researcher? |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| A1 | SiT here = **video diffusion Transformer** with an explicit **time-axis attention block** that is replaceable by a 1-D operator (so \\\"time\\\" = temporal axis of roll-out, not diffusion time / class time). If \\\"time attention\\\" in your question means diffusion-time attention inside SiT, the experiment shape differs. | The whole \\\"non-causal\\\" claim depends on which axis we're mixing. | **Yes — please confirm which axis \\\"time attention\\\" refers to.** |\\n| A2 | \\\"Hyena operator\\\" = Hyena / HyenaDNA-style: gated long convolution + multiplicative gating, parameterised by implicit long kernels (two short convs + element-wise nonlinearity and a positional multiplicative gate). | Influences whether we need a separate explicit long-convolution backbone. | Need confirmation against the specific Hyena variant you have in mind (H3 / Hyena / Hyena-SE / HyenaDNA). |\\n| A3 | \\\"Non-causal\\\" = bidirectional mixing along the time axis (kernel symmetric or forward+backward applied), as opposed to a strictly causal Hyena. | This is what makes the comparison a non-causality test, not just a Hyena vs. attention test. | Please confirm. |\\n| A4 | \\\"Extremely long\\\" sequence length is in the **train-short / eval-long** regime (e.g., train on hundreds of steps, evaluate on thousands) rather than feasibility of training on truly long sequences. | Determines whether the test is about generalisation to longer horizons or about scaling. | **Yes — please define.** |\\n| A5 | \\\"Multiple timescales\\\" can be operationally read as \\\"spectral content spanning several orders of magnitude in period\\\" rather than a specific band count. | Drives the per-band PSD metrics. | Please confirm or pin numerical bands. |\\n| A6 | Equal compute budget in wall-clock terms (not just FLOPs) is acceptable as a control, with FLOPs and params reported. | Without GPU the runtime estimates can't be produced locally; need a runtime target. | **Yes — please state compute budget / wall-clock target.** |\\n| A7 | Compute is **absent in this task** (consistent with `task.json`), so the deliverable is the design document, not trained runs. | Aligns with \\\"do not claim an experiment was run\\\". | Please confirm — environment confirms no GPU and design-only framing. |\\n| A8 | Primary endpoint is a single scalar (or a small, pre-registered set) chosen before seeing results. | Avoids p-hacking / cherry-picking. | **Yes — please pick or approve my candidate.** |\\n| A9 | Sequence elements treated as continuous-valued (not discrete tokens). Hyena on discrete tokens would change the design. | Discrete-token Hyena (HyenaDNA vs. Hyena) has different considerations. | Please confirm continuous-valued sequences. |\\n| A10 | No adversarial / extrapolation beyond the training distribution is required beyond length; covariate shift is not the focus. | Keeps the experimental scope tight. | Please confirm. |\\n\\n### Setup details I cannot infer and cannot resolve without your input\\n\\n1. **Which SiT** (image-video, image-class, or roll-out variant) and **which Hyena variant** — see A1, A2.\\n2. **Specific dataset**: synthetic multi-scale, classical dynamics, motion benchmark, or video. My lean: synthetic + one physical-dynamics system to ground truth the multi-timescale claim independently.\\n3. **Sequence lengths** for training and for the evaluation ladder.\\n4. **Primary endpoint** and pre-registered secondary metrics.\\n5. **Compute budget / training budget** per arm.\\n6. **Inference protocol for non-causal operator** (see non-causality handling above).\\n7. The supplementary topics `neurips2024/93022/6:setup_4` and `neurips2024/93022/6:setup_5` referenced to you — likely two distinct setup interpretations of the same question. **My plan currently blends them into one design; please tell me whether they should be kept as two parallel setups or collapsed.**\\n8. Number of random seeds and statistical-test policy.\\n\\n### Items I will not invent\\n\\n- Specific Hyena kernel sizes, gating parameterisations, or init schemes — I will propose placeholders and ask you to confirm or supply.\\n- Exact sequence lengths and exact dataset choice — pick from a menu above.\\n- A