oll-outs whose short-, medium-, and long-timescale structure all remain consistent.\\n\\n### Two configurations to compare\\n- Arm A \\u2014 SiT-TimeAttn (baseline): SiT's original time attention, as in the source; causality as in original SiT.\\n- Arm B \\u2014 SiT-Hyena (treatment): Hyena-style operator replacing time attention, implemented as implicit long convolutions (two short convs + element-wise MLP + multiplicative gate), non-causal (bidirectional).\\n\\nEverything else kept identical: spatial mixing, conditioning pathway, embedding, optimiser, schedule, batch, seed.\\n\\n### Candidate datasets / trajectory types (pick one or two; needs your call)\\n1. Synthetic multi-timescale sequence \\u2014 a 1-D scalar trajectory with superimposed fast, medium, and slow components from a known SDE/ODE; ground-truth PSD, autocorrelation, and phase coherence are available. Best for isolating \\\"multiple timescales\\\".\\n2. Physical dynamics trajectory \\u2014 classical 2-D / 3-D chaotic or near-chaotic systems (Lorenz-63, double pendulum). Multiscale structure, forecast skill, Lyapunov-style horizon, power spectrum, distributional divergence.\\n3. Moving-MNIST or motion sequences \\u2014 visual qualitative checks plus quantitative FID / FVD on long roll-outs.\\n4. Long-horizon video frames (UCF-101 / Kinetics subset) \\u2014 most aligned with the SiT video lineage but heavier and noisier for a clean ablation.\\n\\nSequence lengths to span \\\"extremely long\\\": an evaluation ladder (e.g. 64 / 256 / 1024 / 4096 steps, or analogous pixel-time horizons) so we can plot degradation and locate where Hyena's sub-quadratic cost diverges from attention.\\n\\n### Candidate primary metrics\\n- Long-horizon consistency: rollout forecast skill (RMSE / NLL) at multiple horizons vs. analytic truth or held-out future; spectral / multi-timescale fidelity (per-band PSD error, log-power-spectrum MSE, autocorrelation consistency at multiple lags).\\n- Multi-timescale fidelity: per-timescale PSD / MSE after band-pass decomposition; phase coherence; Wasserstein-1 on returns.\\n- Computational cost: wall-clock per training step, parameter count, FLOPs, activation memory vs. sequence length.\\n- Training stability: gradient-norm curves, loss curves, NaN / Inf events, effective rank of features.\\n\\nA single scalar primary endpoint (candidate: summed log-PSD error at the longest horizon across timescale bands); others as secondary. Needs your call.\\n\\n### Controls (high-level)\\n- Matched FLOPs as the headline cost-control, with #params reported separately.\\n- Matched training schedule: same optimiser, lr schedule, warm-up, total tokens/steps, batch size, seed, mixed-precision.\\n- Identical conditioning: same noise schedule (DDPM / flow-matching), same conditioning inputs, same sampling procedure (same NFE, same solver).\\n- Identical spatial mixing and everything else except the time-axis operator.\\n- Useful sub-arms if budget allows: causal vs. non-causal Hyena (separates \\\"Hyena\\\" vs. \\\"non-causality\\\"); train-short / eval-long vs. train-long / eval-long.\\n\\n### Sub-questions the experiment should answer\\n1. Does SiT-Hyena match SiT-TimeAttn in multi-timescale fidelity at short horizons?\\n2. Where does their relative error cross over as horizon grows?\\n3. Is SiT-Hyena's per-step cost genuinely sub-quadratic and does the gap widen with length?\\n4. Does non-causality cause any pathology at inference (boundary effects from bidirectional mixing at generation time)?\\n5. Is training stability comparable between arms?\\n\\n### Non-causality handling at inference (flag, needs explicit design)\\nAt generation time, a non-causal operator cannot strictly use future frames. Candidate protocols: (i) two-pass rollout with a buffer of past+future predictions and a substitution rule; (ii) streaming with a fixed future window; (iii) teacher-forcing-style chunked rollouts. This choice can dominate the comparison and must be fixed identically across arms. Needs your call.\\n\\n## (3) Assumptions and uncertainties\\n\\n### Consequential assumptions I would need to make to finalise the design\\n\\n| # | Assumption | Why it matters | Resolvable only by researcher? |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| A1 | SiT's \\\"time attention\\\" is the time-axis temporal mixer (not diffusion-time attention) | The \\\"non-causal\\\" claim depends on which axis we mix | Yes \\u2014 please confirm axis |\\n| A2 | \\\"Hyena operator\\\" = Hyena / HyenaDNA-style gated long-convolution mixer (not H3) | Influences whether a separate long-convolution backbone is needed | Needs variant confirmation |\\n| A3 | \\\"Non-causal\\\" = bidirectional mixing along the time axis | What makes the comparison a non-causality test vs. just Hyena-vs-attention | Please confirm |\\n| A4 | \\\"Extremely long\\\" = train-short / eval-long regime | Determines whether the test is about generalisation-to-long-horizon or scaling | Yes \\u2014 please define |\\n| A5 | \\\"Multiple timescales\\\" = spectral content spanning several orders of magnitude in period | Drives per-band PSD metrics | Please confirm or pin bands |\\n| A6 | Equal wall-clock compute is acceptable as a control, with FLOPs and params reported | Without GPU the runtime cannot be estimated locally | Yes \\u2014 please state budget |\\n| A7 | Compute is absent (consistent with task.json); deliverable is the design document | Aligns with \\\"do not claim an experiment was run\\\" | Please confirm |\\n| A8 | Primary endpoint is a single scalar (or a small pre-registered set) | Avoids p-hacking / cherry-picking | Yes \\u2014 please pick or approve my candidate |\\n| A9 | Sequence elements are continuous-valued (not discrete tokens) | Discrete-token Hyena changes considerations | Please confirm |\\n| A10 | No adverse-distribution extrapolation beyond length is required | Keeps scope tight | Please confirm |\\n\\n### Setup details I cannot infer\\n1. Which SiT (video diffusion Transformer, image-class di