{"id": "57f02c8d-4ede-4cc5-b08d-c58dfedd67e3", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T14:29:09.812859", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "2873147e-ef77-430d-9738-45ee83b7ffd8", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-6ae9ae20-9cd0-45d8-9f6f-a9224531d714", "tool_call": {"id": "call-6ae9ae20-9cd0-45d8-9f6f-a9224531d714", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Full design result document with formulas, controls, stats, risks\",\"stage\":\"result\",\"answer\":\"## Experiment design — SiT × non-causal Hyena time operator for long, multi-timescale trajectories\\n\\n**Deliverable type.** Design document only. No experiment is executed in this sandbox; the environment has no GPU, no Torch, and no network. The single execution-grade plan item included here is a wiring-sanity check, listed as a future step with an explicit caveat that it is not a reported result of this study.\\n\\n**Central question (locked).** Does replacing the time-axis attention mechanism with a non-causal Hyena operator in the SiT architecture allow the model to generate extremely long, consistent trajectories that capture dynamics over multiple timescales?\\n\\n### 0. Separation of fixed task requirements from design assumptions\\n\\nThis document distinguishes (i) requirements fixed by the task itself from (ii) design assumptions made to instantiate the experiment. Both are required to interpret any eventual result.\\n\\n- **Fixed task requirements (not negotiable in this design).**\\n  - Replace SiT's time-axis operator with a non-causal Hyena-family operator.\\n  - Compare against SiT with its original time-axis attention.\\n  - Trajectories must be long and must capture dynamics across multiple timescales.\\n  - \\\"Consistency\\\" applies to the generated trajectory itself.\\n  - Deliverable is a design document (not a report of a run).\\n  - Public task scope: design (track.md: \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\\\").\\n- **Design assumptions (locked here from the researcher's resolutions; see §10).**\\n  - Time axis = the roll-out / temporal axis of the generated trajectory, not diffusion time.\\n  - Hyena variant = standard Hyena-family operator (two short convolutions + element-wise multiplicative gating + implicit long convolution via FFT), instantiated bidirectionally.\\n  - Datasets = synthetic multi-timescale sequences (≥3 decades of period) plus Lorenz-63.\\n  - Length ladder = 64 / 256 / 1024 / 4096, evaluated identically in both arms.\\n  - Inference = chunked two-pass rollout with a fixed future-window substitution rule, applied identically across arms.\\n  - Cost control = matched FLOPs; #params and activation memory reported; wall-clock not claimed.\\n  - Primary endpoint = summed log-PSD error at the longest horizon across N=3 timescale bands.\\n  - Stats = ≥3 random seeds per arm, paired by seed, paired bootstrap 95% CIs, pre-registration before any (hypothetical) observation.\\n  - Two-arm unified design; optionally a clearly labelled causal-Hyena sub-arm as secondary, not primary.\\n  - Sequence elements are continuous-valued.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 1. Hypothesis and rationale\\n\\n**Working hypothesis (informal).** A non-causal Hyena operator, by allowing the model to read both past and future along the roll-out axis within a buffered window during training and inference, can preserve low-frequency (long-timescale) structure of the underlying dynamics further into the horizon than causal / unidirectional time attention, without sacrificing high-frequency fidelity, and at matched FLOPs.\\n\\n**Formal pre-registered hypothesis.** Let $L_{\\\\text{primary}}(T;\\\\,\\\\theta)$ be the primary endpoint (summed log-PSD error at horizon $T$; see §5) computed for arm $\\\\theta\\\\in\\\\{A,B\\\\}$ at $T=4096$. The pre-registered alternative is a paired cross-seed comparison at $T=4096$:\\n\\n- $H_0$: $\\\\mathbb{E}[\\\\,L_{\\\\text{primary}}(4096;\\\\,B)\\\\,]\\\\,=\\\\,\\\\mathbb{E}[\\\\,L_{\\\\text{primary}}(4096;\\\\,A)\\\\,]$ (Hyena and Attention are equivalent at the longest horizon).\\n- $H_1$ (directional, pre-specified): $\\\\mathbb{E}[\\\\,L_{\\\\text{primary}}(4096;\\\\,B)\\\\,]\\\\,<\\\\,\\\\mathbb{E}[\\\\,L_{\\\\text{primary}}(4096;\\\\,A)\\\\,]$ (Hyena has lower summed log-PSD error at the longest horizon).\\n\\nA two-tailed 95% paired-bootstrap CI on $L_{\\\\text{primary}}(B)-L_{\\\\text{primary}}(A)$ will be reported; rejection of $H_0$ requires the CI to exclude 0 with the directional sign of $H_1$. The same comparison at shorter horizons (64, 256, 1024) is reported on the evaluation ladder (§6) but is not the primary endpoint.\\n\\n**Rationale for choosing this as primary.** The PSD on a logarithmic scale is the most directly interpretable diagnostic of multi-timescale fidelity: each decade of period contributes a distinct band, and the log-domain error penalises order-of-magnitude distortions that linear RMSE would dilute. The longest horizon (4096) is where the failure mode the question is about — loss of long-timescale coherence — should manifest most strongly if it exists, and where the cost gap between attention (quadratic in $T$) and Hyena (sub-quadratic via FFT long convolution) should be visible.\\n\\n**Why a non-causal Hyena might help.** Bidirectional neighbourhood context lets the operator resolve local ambiguity (e.g. phase and amplitude of a fast oscillation given its slower envelope) using immediate neighbours; inattention this can be recovered only across many causal steps, where compounding error depresses long-timescale coherence. The matched-FLOPs control ensures any advantage is not a parameter-budget artefact.\\n\\n**Why it might not.** Non-causal mixing at inference requires a future-window substitution rule, which both architectures share, and this rule may dominate the comparison; the gain at long horizons may trade off against local fidelity; or the bottleneck may be elsewhere in the model (spatial mixing, conditioning) rather than the time operator.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2. Treatment structure\\n\\n#### 2.1 Primary arms (the comparison the question asks about)\\n\\n- **Arm A — SiT-TimeAttn (baseline).** SiT with its original time-axis attention. Causality flag is whatever the original SiT specification uses (typically causal / causal-masked); that flag is recorded and held fixed for Arm A. All other components identical to Arm B. This arm is the reference point.\\n- **Arm B — SiT-Hyena (treatment).** SiT with the time-axis attention replaced by the Hyena-family operator defined below. This arm is the reference point for the answer to the question.\\n\\nThe remaining parts of the architecture (spatial mixing, conditioning, embeddings, normalisation, output head) are identical between A and B and are listed under controlled variables (§3). Downstream sampling, noise schedule, and NFE are likewise identical.\\n\\n**Hyena time operator — exact specification.** Per the assumption lock-in, the operator applied along the roll-out axis of each token at every SiT block is:\\n\\n1. Two short 1-D convolutions (suggested kernel sizes $k_1=3$, $k_2=3$) producing intermediate features $u$, $v$ along the time axis.\\n2. Element-wise multiplicative gate $g=\\\\sigma(\\\\text{Linear}(u))\\\\odot v$, where $\\\\sigma$ is a configurable nonlinearity (default GELU).\\n3. Implicit long convolution via FFT: project the gated signal with an implicitly parameterised long kernel and multiply by FFT, then inverse FFT. Kernel parameterisation follows Hyena-family practice (e.g., two short MLPs parametrising a small set of values that are then interpolated / windowed to length $T$, with truncation to enforce nonnegativity if the source variant requires it).\\n4. **Non-causal** meaning bidirectional along the time axis: no future masking and symmetric padding for the short convolutions; the FFT long convolution is applied as a pointwise product in the frequency domain and is therefore naturally non-causal along the time axis.\\n\\nThese defaults are placeholders to be locked at wiring-sanity time (§9); the design's conclusions do not depend on a specific kernel size, only on the architectural family.\\n\\n#### 2.2 Optional secondary sub-arm (clearly labelled, not a primary endpoint)\\n\\n- **Arm B′ — SiT-Hyena-Causal.** Same as B with a strictly causal Hyena (asymmetric padding on the short convolutions and either one-sided FFT windowing or an extra triangular mask placeholder to be filled at wiring time). This arm separates the \\\"Hyena vs. attention\\\" effect from the \\\"non-causality vs. causality\\\" effect, so a Hyena-arm gain in the primary endpoint can be attributed correctly. This is reported as a clearly secondary analysis and is not pre-registered as a primary endpoint.\\n\\n#### 2.3 What is not an arm\\n\\n- A causal-attention sub-arm is **not** required for the question asked and is left out of the unified design; if the researcher later wants an extra sanity check, it can be added without changing the primary endpoint. The decision excludes it from the unified design is recorded in §10 so the omission is reversible.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 3. Controlled variables (held fixed across both arms)\\n\\nThe following are explicitly held constant between Arm A and Arm B (and Arm B′ unless flagged otherwise). Each item can be verified by inspecting the configuration diff between arms, which should differ only in the time-axis operator.\\n\\n**Architecture (outside the time operator).**\\n- Spatial mixing block (operator, depth, width).\\n- Cross-axis / conditioning pathway (class, text, time-of-diffusion, etc., as in source).\\n- Token / patch / positional embeddings (including any rotary / ALiBi / sinusoidal parameters).\\n- Normalisation placement and type.\\n- Output head.\\n- Number of SiT blocks, embedding dimension, attention/operator width.\\n\\n**Optimisation and schedule.**\\n- Optimiser (AdamW; β₁=0.9, β₂=0.95; weight-decay value fixed).\\n- Learning-rate schedule (peak value, warmup length in steps, decay shape).\\n- Total training tokens, total optimiser steps, batch size, gradient accumulation.\\n- Mixed-precision policy (dtype for params / activations / reductions).\\n- Gradient clipping value.\\n- Dropout rates.\\n- Random seeds for data sampling, parameter init, dropout.\\n\\n**Diffusion and sampling.**\\n- Noise schedule (cosine / linear / flow-matching parameter, fixed once chosen).\\n- Sampling procedure: same solver (e.g. DDPM / flow-matching Euler / Heun), same number of function evaluations (NFE), same step-size schedule.\\n- Identical inference protocol (chunked two-pass rollout with fixed future-window substitution; §4), applied identically across arms.\\n\\n**Data.**\\n- Preprocessing pipeline (normalisation, channel ordering, clipping).\\n- Training sequence length (kept at a single value within the \\\"train-short\\\" regime; evaluation uses the length ladder).\\n- Train / validation / test split (fixed across arms).\\n- Held-out ground truth for evaluation: analytic closed-form for the synthetic generator; numerical integration at fine resolution for Lorenz-63 (§5 and §6).\\n\\n**Cost control.**\\n- Matched FLOPs at forward pass level, evaluated at a reference sequence length equal to the training length.\\n- #params and activation memory (peak bytes per forward pass) are reported separately and not part of the cost-control target.\\n- Wall-clock is not claimed as an endpoint in this design-only deliverable.\\n\\n**Hardware and software.**\\n- Single accelerator target (one GPU type, to be specified at run time).\\n- Single software stack (framework version, CUDA, library versions).\\n- Determinism flags where supported.\\n\\nThe single item explicitly *allowed to vary* between arms is the time-axis operator (Arm A's attention vs. Arm B's non-causal Hyena). The optional sub-arm B′ further varies causal/non-causal masking within the Hyena family.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 4. Inference protocol for non-causality — applied identically across arms\\n\\n**Chunked two-pass rollout with a fixed future-window substitution rule.** At generation time the model produces chunks of length $C$ (suggested default $C=128$), with a fixed future-window size $W$ (suggested default $W=64$) used to buffer the next $W$ predicted steps. Within each chunk the time-axis operator sees both past context and the buffered future, exactly as it did at training time. After producing each chunk, the window is shifted: the first $C$ produced steps are kept as the roll-out, the buffered $W$ future steps are discarded and replaced by the next chunk's predictions.\\n\\nThe protocol is applied **identically** in Arms A, B, and B′, with the same $C$ and $W$, the same chunk boundaries, and the same substitution rule. Within a chunk, the Hyena operator runs bidirectionally as designed; the attention baseline performs attention over the same buffered span with the same masking rules as in training. By equating the windowed span between arms, the protocol does not systematically favour one operator over the other.\\n\\nThis choice removes the strongest design ambiguity around \\\"non-causal at inference\\\": whatever bidirectional lookahead Hyena can exploit is matched by the same buffer length being available to attention within each chunk.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 5. Datasets and ground truth\\n\\n#### 5.1 Synthetic multi-timescale trajectories (primary benchmark)\\n\\nGenerator: a deterministic continuous-time signal $x(t)$ constructed as a sum of three (or more) components spanning $\\\\geq 3$ decades of characteristic period. A canonical choice:\\n\\n$x(t) = A_s \\\\sin(2\\\\pi t/T_s + \\\\phi_s) + A_m \\\\sin(2\\\\pi t/T_m + \\\\phi_m) + x_\\\\ell(t)$\\n\\nwhere $T_s=1$, $T_m=100$, $\\\\ell$ corresponds to a slow component with characteristic period $T_\\\\ell \\\\geq 10^4$; $x_\\\\ell(t)$ may be a slow sinusoid or a slow stochastic drift drawn from a separate SDE. Phases $\\\\phi_s,\\\\phi_m$ are fixed per generated sample to permit per-sample phase-coherence metrics. Realisations are produced at a sampling rate commensurate with the shortest component (≥ 10 samples per shortest period) and discretised.\\n\\n**Ground truth available analytically:**\\n- Closed-form power spectral density $P_x(f)$ as a sum of delta peaks for sinusoidal components plus an analytic PSD for the slow component.\\n- Closed-form autocorrelation $R_x(\\\\tau)$.\\n- Closed-form phase structure (instantaneous amplitude and frequency).\\n\\nThis is the primary benchmark because every key property in the central question (\\\"consistent\\\", \\\"long\\\", \\\"multiple timescales\\\") admits an analytic ground truth, eliminating estimator-induced ambiguity in the primary endpoint.\\n\\n#### 5.2 Lorenz-63 (grounding in physical dynamics)\\n\\nGenerator: the Lorenz-63 SDE (deterministic ODE) at standard parameters ($\\\\sigma=10, \\\\rho=28, \\\\beta=8/3$), integrated with a fine-step RK4 (or equivalent) for both training data and ground-truth trajectories, with a small integration step (e.g. $\\\\Delta t = 0.005$ in Lorenz time units) and downsampled to the discretisation rate used for evaluation. Each trajectory's $(x(t), y(t), z(t))$ is treated as a multi-channel signal; PSD and other metrics are computed per channel and averaged.\\n\\n**Ground truth:** held-out fine-resolution numerical integration, not analytic formulas (Lorenz has no closed-form PSD). The horizon ladder uses integration-time units commensurate with the chosen discretisation rate.\\n\\nLorenz-63 is included because its multi-timescale structure (fast orbital timescale vs. slow switching between lobes) is well-studied and physically interpretable; success or failure on it discriminates from the synthetic case in a meaningful way.\\n\\n#### 5.3 Across datasets\\n- \\\"Multiple timescales\\\" is operationalised as $\\\\geq 3$ decade-separated period bands (short / medium / long).\\n- \\\"Extremely long\\\" is operationalised as the 4096-step evaluation horizon (§6), with shorter horizons retained for diagnostic purposes.\\n\\n#### 5.4 What is intentionally out of scope in this design\\n- Image-video SiT (e.g. UCF-101 / Kinetics long-horizon video). This is excluded to keep the primary endpoint on analytically-grounded trajectories; the design can be extended later but the unified design here does not run it.\\n- Long-horizon language / discrete-token trajectories. Excluded by the assumption lock-in that sequence elements are continuous-valued.\\n- Real-world motion-capture benchmarks. Excluded; the synthetic and Lorenz design already covers the demands of the central question.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 6. Evaluation ladder and protocol\\n\\n**Sequence lengths:** 64 / 256 / 1024 / 4096 steps. Each length is evaluated identically in both arms using the same protocol (§4) with the same chunk size $C$ and future window $W$. The training length is held fixed within the train-short regime and is reused across arms.\\n\\n**Per-length, per-seed evaluation produces:**\\n- Generated roll-out $\\\\hat x^{(s)}_T[s=1,\\\\dots,T]$ for each seed $s$.\\n- Pointwise deterministic forecast skill versus held-out ground truth.\\n- Spectral estimates and band-decomposed errors.\\n- Cost figures at forward-pass level for the chosen $T$.\\n\\n**What varies across lengths vs. across seeds:**\\n- Length varies as the ladder dimension: 64, 256, 1024, 4096.\\n- Seed varies as the randomness dimension: minimum 3 seeds per arm per length per dataset.\\n\\n**Sequence-length ladder as a diagnostic graph.** The design's intent is that primary conclusions be drawn at $T=4096$, with the shorter horizons used to characterise degradation curves and to locate any cross-over length at which the relative ordering of A and B reverses. The hypothesis pre-registered in §1 commits to the longest horizon; the ladder exists for transparency and reproducibility.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 7. Endpoints with exact formulas\\n\\nNotation. Let $\\\\hat x = (\\\\hat x[1],\\\\dots,\\\\hat x[T])$ be a single generated trajectory, and $x^\\\\* = (x^\\\\*[1],\\\\dots,x^\\\\*[T])$ be the corresponding ground-truth trajectory. For multi-channel data (Lorenz), endpoints are computed per channel and averaged. Welch's PSD with window length $W_\\\\text{welch}$ (referenced as $W_\\\\text{welch}$, distinct from the future-window $W$) and 50% overlap is the default PSD estimator; parameters are fixed across arms.\\n\\n**Pre-registration note.** The endpoints below, including all formulas, the log-PSD convention, the band partition, the forecast horizons, and the set of autocorrelation lags, are written down *before* any data is examined. They are locked here as part of the design document.\\n\\n#### 7.1 Primary endpoint\\n\\n$$\\\\boxed{L_{\\\\text{primary}}(T=4096) \\\\;=\\\\; \\\\sum_{b\\\\in\\\\{\\\\text{short, medium, long}\\\\}} \\\\frac{1}{|B_b|} \\\\sum_{f\\\\in B_b}\\\\bigl(\\\\log \\\\hat P(f) - \\\\log P^\\\\*(f)\\\\bigr)^2}$$\\n\\nwhere $\\\\hat P(f)$ is the PSD estimator of $\\\\hat x$, $P^\\\\*(f)$ is the ground-truth PSD (analytic for synthetic; estimator of held-out fine integration for Lorenz), and $\\\\{B_b\\\\}_b$ is a fixed partition of the frequency axis into three bands aligned to the period decades of the generator (e.g. for synthetic: $B_\\\\text{short} = \\\\{f: f \\\\in [f_s/2,\\\\,2f_s]\\\\}$, $B_\\\\text{medium} = \\\\{f: f \\\\in [f_m/2,\\\\,2f_m]\\\\}$, $B_\\\\text{long} = \\\\{f: f \\\\in [\\\\tfrac{1}{2T},\\\\,f_\\\\ell/2]\\\\}$, with $f_s, f_m, f_\\\\ell$ derived from $T_s, T_m, T_\\\\ell$). The log-domain MSE in each band is summed across bands. Lower is better.\\n\\n**Why this works.** It directly probes multi-timescale structure (one term per decade), uses a long horizon ($T=4096$) so the long-timescale band demands information over thousands of steps, and is shape-sensitive in log space (an order-of-magnitude PSD error in any band is penalised in proportion to its squared magnitude).\\n\\n#### 7.2 Secondary endpoints\\n\\n1. **Forecast RMSE and NLL at multiple horizons.** Let $\\\\mathcal{H}\\\\subseteq\\\\{16, 64, 256, 1024\\\\}$ be fixed evaluation horizons during roll-out (avoid $T$ itself so the endpoint is on the *trajectory*, not at the end-point only).\\n\\n$$\\\\text{RMSE}(h) \\\\;=\\\\; \\\\sqrt{\\\\frac{1}{N_s N_h}\\\\sum_{s=1}^{N_s}\\\\sum_{t:\\\\,t+h\\\\in[1,T]}\\\\bigl(\\\\hat x^{(s)}[t+h] - x^{\\\\*(s)}[t+h]\\\\bigr)^2}$$\\n\\n$$\\\\text{NLL}(h) \\\\;=\\\\; -\\\\frac{1}{N_s N_h}\\\\sum_{s,t}\\\\log p_{\\\\theta_s}(\\\\hat x^{(s)}[t+h] \\\\mid \\\\hat x^{(s)}[\\\\leq t])$$\\n\\nwith the predictive density $p_\\\\theta$ parameterised by the model head at training time; if the head is deterministic, NLL is replaced by a Gaussian-fit residual NLL as a clearly-labelled degradation. RMSE and NLL are reported per horizon.\\n\\n2. **Autocorrelation consistency at multiple lags.** $\\\\mathcal{L}\\\\subseteq\\\\{1, 8, 64, 512\\\\}$ lags. Per trajectory:\\n\\n$$\\\\text{AC}_\\\\tau(\\\\hat x) = \\\\frac{\\\\sum_{t=1}^{T-\\\\tau}(\\\\hat x[t]-\\\\bar{\\\\hat x})(\\\\hat x[t+\\\\tau]-\\\\bar{\\\\hat x})}{\\\\sum_{t=1}^{T}(\\\\hat x[t]-\\\\bar{\\\\hat x})^2},\\\\qquad \\\\text{AC}_\\\\tau(x^\\\\*)\\\\text{ analogously.}$$\\n\\nThe endpoint is $\\\\text{AC-MSE} = \\\\frac{1}{|\\\\mathcal{L}|}\\\\sum_{\\\\tau\\\\in\\\\mathcal{L}}\\\\bigl(\\\\text{AC}_\\\\tau(\\\\hat x) - \\\\text{AC}_\\\\tau(x^\\\\*)\\\\bigr)^2$, per seed, then averaged.\\n\\n3. **Wasserstein-1 on increments.** Distribution of $\\\\Delta_t \\\\hat x = \\\\hat x[t+1]-\\\\hat x[t]$ vs. $\\\\Delta_t x^\\\\*$. Reported as $W_1(\\\\hat\\\\Delta,\\\\Delta^\\\\*)$ using the empirical one-dimensional Wasserstein-1 with linear interpolation. Sensitive to distributional drift; complementary to AC-MSE.\\n\\n4. **Training stability.** Per training run, recorded are: (i) maximum and final values of the gradient norm; (ii) loss-curve shape (smoothed training loss at fixed iteration milestones); (iii) NaN/Inf event count; (iv) effective rank of the penultimate-block activations on a held-out batch (mean across a small calibration set). Each is a scalar reported per seed.\\n\\n5. **Scale of cost curve (FLOPs and activation memory).** Analytical estimate of forward FLOPs as a function of $T\\\\in\\\\{64,256,1024,4096\\\\}$ for each arm, and empirical activation memory estimate at the training sequence length. Both are reported on the same axes as supporting evidence, not as components of the cost-control target.\\n\\nThe secondary endpoints do not affect acceptance / rejection of $H_0$; they support interpretation of the primary result (where any effect comes from, whether it tracks forecast horizon, autocorrelation lag, distributional shift, training stability, or cost).\\n\\n#### 7.3 What is intentionally not an endpoint in this design\\n- Wall-clock training time (no GPU / no execution in the sandbox; wall-clock not claimed as part of result).\\n- Qualitative perceptual scores for held-out humans (out of scope for this design-only deliverable).\\n- Any metric computed on a held-out distinct dataset (e.g. Internet video). The dataset choice is locked to synthetic + Lorenz.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 8. Statistical analysis plan\\n\\n**Pre-registration.** The primary endpoint, the directional alternative $H_1$ (§1), the seed count, the bootstrap resample count, and the bootstrap CI definition are locked in this design document before any execution. No test was performed in this sandbox; pre-registration here applies to any future execution of this design.\\n\\n**Seeds.** Minimum $N_\\\\text{seeds}=3$ per arm, per dataset, per evaluation length, paired by seed (i.e. seed $s$ for Arm A and seed $s$ for Arm B share their data-sampling seed and differ only in operator). Increasing $N_\\\\text{seeds}$ is encouraged if budget allows; the minimum is the smallest count that yields a paired bootstrap CI with practical width. The seed count is part of pre-registration.\\n\\n**Paired comparison.** For each arm, $\\\\theta\\\\in\\\\{A,B\\\\}$, compute $\\\\ell_s^{(\\\\theta)} = L_{\\\\text{primary}}^{(s)}(\\\\theta)$ at $T=4096$, where $s\\\\in\\\\{1,\\\\dots,N_\\\\text{seeds}\\\\}$ indexes the seed. The paired difference is $d_s = \\\\ell_s^{(B)} - \\\\ell_s^{(A)}$. The summary statistic of interest is $\\\\bar d$ (mean difference).\\n\\n**Bootstrap CI.** Resample seed index $s\\\\in\\\\{1,\\\\dots,N_\\\\text{seeds}\\\\}$ with replacement $B_\\\\text{boot}=10{,}000$ times; for each resample compute $\\\\bar d^\\\\*$. The 95% CI is the percentile interval $(\\\\bar d^\\\\*_{(0.025)}, \\\\bar d^\\\\*_{(0.975)})$. Reject $H_0$ in favour of the directional $H_1$ iff the entire CI lies below zero. Otherwise, report the CI and the point estimate.\\n\\n**Multiplicity.** The ladder §6 produces $L_{\\\\text{primary}}$ at four lengths, but only $T=4096$ is the pre-registered primary; the shorter lengths are reported as the secondary diagnostic $L_\\\\text{short}$, $L_{256}$, $L_{1024}$, but no multiplicity correction is pre-applied and they are not used for acceptance / rejection.\\n\\n**Secondary endpoints.** Reported with the same paired-bootstrap structure, sign of effect recorded but no rejection criterion pre-registered. Band-level decomposition of the primary endpoint is reported as a decomposition, not as separate endpoints.\\n\\n**Effect-size reporting.** Always report the point estimate, the 95% CI, and the matched-FLOPs configuration under which it was obtained. Do not report $p$-values as the primary inferential quantity; the bootstrap CI is the inferential quantity.\\n\\n**Missing-data handling.** If a seed's run fails (NaN, divergence), the seed is replaced by another seed up to a maximum of $N_\\\\text{seeds}+2$ runs per arm; failed runs are recorded and reported.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 9. Feasibility checks\\n\\n**Wiring-sanity plan (the only execution-grade check that could in principle be performed outside this sandbox).** A small smoke run, not a result of the study, would be performed in a real execution environment to confirm that both arms are wired correctly before any production training begins. It is listed here as a plan, not a result. The actual check is *not* performed in this sandbox; this design-only deliverable does not claim any run succeeded.\\n\\n- **Sanity data.** A tiny trajectory of length $T_{\\\\text{train}}=256$, generated by a single-band sinusoid + small noise, with analytic ground truth.\\n- **Sanity schedule.** Very short training (a few hundred optimiser steps), small batch, no scheduling tricks; identical hyperparameters across arms except the time operator.\\n- **Sanity check.** After training, the model is asked to denoise a freshly initialised noisy real trajectory and produce a qualitative plot. The expected behaviour is that both arms reproduce the sinusoid shape with phase approximately preserved and amplitude close to the true value, up to under-training tolerance. Failure looks like a flat output, a constant mean, a phase-shift inversion, or a divergent curve.\\n- **What this does not establish.** Wiring-sanity does not establish that the primary endpoint shows a treatment difference, nor that the result generalises beyond the sanity-task distribution.\\n- **No run is reported here.** This deliverable is the design; no execution is claimed or performed.\\n\\n**Design-side feasibility checks (those that *can* be performed in this sandbox, no execution of the experiment).**\\n- Workspace inspection: confirmed empty of SiT/Hyena materials (§11). Therefore the architecture's reported parameter counts and cost figures cannot be empirically validated locally.\\n- Environment inspection: confirmed Python 3.12.3, 24 CPUs, no GPU, no Torch, no network (§11). Therefore no part of training, sampling, or evaluation can be executed locally.\\n- Pre-registration mechanics: confirmed each formula and each pre-registered step (seed count, bootstrap resample count, endpoint definition, ladder, dataset, protocol) is stated in this document and reproducible from §6–§8 alone.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 10. Residual risks and conditions that would invalidate the design's conclusions\\n\\nThe conclusions of any future run of this design would be **invalidated** under any of the following conditions; each is a residual risk, with a brief description of why it threatens the conclusion and what would mitigate it.\\n\\n1. **Matched-FLOPs recipe material-change risk.** Setting matched FLOPs to be exactly equal between A and B forces width / depth trade-offs (e.g. wider but shallower Hyena) that could themselves drive any observed difference. Conclusion invalidated if the recipe silently changes another component. Mitigation: report and constrain depth / width bounds explicitly so the recipe cannot reach a degenerate configuration; include a sensitivity over at least one alternative matched-FLOPs recipe (e.g. constant depth, varying width).\\n2. **Inference-protocol favouritism risk.** The chunked two-pass rollout with future-window substitution gives the buffered future to *both* arms. If the buffering interacts unexpectedly with one operator but not the other (e.g. masking conventions for causal attention inside a buffered span), the protocol could favour one arm non-trivially. Conclusion invalidated if the protocol's effects are not symmetric. Mitigation: verify the symmetry by an ablation of buffer size ($W\\\\in\\\\{0,32,64,128\\\\}$); if attention's relative performance collapses for $W=0$ vs. $W=128$ but Hyena's does not, revisit the protocol.\\n3. **Synthetic-saturation risk.** If both arms reach essentially the analytic ground-truth on the synthetic dataset by $T=1024$, the experiment may fail to discriminate at $T=4096$. Conclusion invalidated only if the failure is silent (no diagnostics). Mitigation: report band-decomposed decomposition at all ladder steps; report a noise-robustness stress test (PSNR of generated trajectories under added input noise).\\n4. **Lorenz-63 idiosyncrasy risk.** The fast / slow separation in Lorenz is specific (orbital vs. lobe-switching), so behaviour on it may not generalise to other physical systems. Conclusion invalidated if the design's claim is interpreted too broadly. Mitigation: report band definitions and per-band decomposition so the result on Lorenz is interpretable as \\\"fast vs. switching\\\", not as a universal claim; document the limits of inference in the discussion.\\n5. **PSD-estimator bias risk at long horizon.** Welch on $T=4096$ with default settings biases the low-frequency band toward higher variance than the analytic formula; bias is unstable across seeds. Conclusion on the **long** band could be unreliable even if the headline number is statistically significant elsewhere. Mitigation: enforce a fixed Welch configuration across arms, report the band-level $L_b$ values alongside the sum, and require the long-band effect to be consistent in sign across at least two PSD-estimator settings (e.g. Welch and Bartlett).\\n6. **Low-seed detection-power risk.** $N_\\\\text{seeds}=3$ with bootstrap CIs produces wide intervals; the design may fail to detect a real effect (false negative) even if one exists. Conclusion *of non-inferiority* would be weakened; conclusion *of superiority* would be unwarranted. Mitigation: report effect size and CI width explicitly; do not infer \\\"no effect\\\" from an underpowered comparison.\\n7. **Optional causal sub-arm risk.** The causal-Hyena sub-arm uses a non-standard causal mask on the FFT long convolution (or one-sided padding on the short convolutions). If this masking is incorrectly implemented, the sub-arm's interpretation collapses. Conclusion of the secondary causal-vs-non-causal decomposition would be invalidated. Mitigation: causal sub-arm is explicitly labelled secondary; its failure does not invalidate the primary arm comparison.\\n8. **Pre-registration scope risk.** The pre-registration locks the primary endpoint, the primary horizon, the seed count, and the bootstrap definition. It does *not* lock corrections for restarts, hyperparameter re-tuning, or non-seed-conditioned learning-rate re-tuning. If post-hoc tuning is allowed, the design's type-I error is uncontrolled. Mitigation: a separate tuning budget for which results are not part of the primary inference must be declared at design time (e.g. \\\"10% of compute reserved for tuning, not in reported results\\\").\\n9. **Wall-clock absence risk.** Because wall-clock is not claimed in this design-only deliverable, claims about practical efficiency are out of scope. Any extension to claim wall-clock benefits would require a separate, environment-specific design. Conclusion invalidated if claimed. Mitigation: report FLOPs and activation memory analytically (no execution); explicitly mark any extension as out of scope.\\n10. **Concept-of-\\\"consistent\\\" ambiguity risk.** The word \\\"consistent\\\" can be read as (i) trajectory-level fidelity (close to ground truth point by point), (ii) distributional fidelity (marginals and derived statistics match), or (iii) self-consistency across the roll-out (no drift, no artefacts, no mode collapse). The primary endpoint addresses (i) and (ii); the ladder and Wasserstein-1 supplement (ii); self-consistency in sense (iii) is addressed only indirectly via the long-horizon structural metrics. Conclusion invalidated if \\\"consistent\\\" is interpreted in a sense not covered by the metrics. Mitigation: report long-run moments of $\\\\hat x$ (drift, variance, kurtosis) and a low-order Wasserstein-1 on the empirical marginal distribution as clearly-labelled supporting evidence.\\n11. **Sandbox-execution impossibility risk.** No part of training, sampling, evaluation, or wiring-sanity has been executed in the present sandbox; any claim that \\\"the design's predictions were confirmed\\\" cannot be made from this deliverable. Mitigation: this design document explicitly disclaims execution.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 11. Repository observations (evidence)\\n\\n- `/workspace` at the time of design contains only `task.json` (511 bytes), which restates the question and the note `documents.track.md = \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"` No SiT, Hyena, or trajectory-modelling references exist locally.\\n- `$HOME`, `/tmp`, `/data` are empty of any relevant material.\\n- No topical directory (SiT / Hyena / paper / repo) is reachable anywhere up to depth 3.\\n- Python 3.12.3 with 24 CPU cores available; no GPU (nvidia-smi cannot communicate with a driver); no torch installed; network disabled per task spec.\\n- These observations are consistent with `task.json` and with the design-only nature of the deliverable.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 12. Summary of the locked design in one screen\\n\\n| Item | Value |\\n|---|---|\\n| Arms | A — SiT-TimeAttn (baseline); B — SiT-Hyena non-causal (treatment). |\\n| Optional sub-arm | B′ — SiT-Hyena-Causal (secondary, not primary). |\\n| Datasets | Synthetic multi-timescale (analytic ground truth, ≥3 decade bands); Lorenz-63 (held-out fine-integration ground truth). |\\n| Sequence elements | Continuous-valued. |\\n| Time axis | Roll-out / temporal axis (not diffusion-time). |\\n| Length ladder | 64 / 256 / 1024 / 4096, identical across arms. |\\n| Training / eval regime | Train-short, eval-long. |\\n| Inference protocol | Chunked two-pass rollout, future window $W$ identical across arms. |\\n| Cost control | Matched FLOPs; #params and activation memory reported separately; wall-clock not claimed. |\\n| Primary endpoint | $\\\\displaystyle L_{\\\\text{primary}}(T=4096) = \\\\sum_b \\\\frac{1}{|B_b|}\\\\sum_{f\\\\in B_b}(\\\\log\\\\hat P(f)-\\\\log P^\\\\*(f))^2$ across 3 decade bands. |\\n| Secondary endpoints | Forecast RMSE / NLL at horizons 16, 64, 256, 1024; AC-MSE at lags 1, 8, 64, 512; Wasserstein-1 on increments; training-stability scalars; FLOPs/activation cost curve. |\\n| Pre-registration | Locked at design time: primary endpoint, primary horizon, seed count, bootstrap count, CI definition. |\\n| Statistical test | Paired bootstrap CI on $L_{\\\\text{primary}}(B)-L_{\\\\text{primary}}(A)$ at $T=4096$, 95% CI, $B_\\\\text{boot}=10{,}000$, $\\\\geq 3$ seeds paired by seed. |\\n| Hypotheses | $H_0$: equal; $H_1$: Hyena lower one-sided, CI-excludes-0 criterion. |\\n| Controls (held fixed) | Spatial mixing, conditioning, embeddings, optimiser, schedule, batch, seed, noise schedule, sampling, NFE, solver, preprocessing, hardware, software, training length. |\\n| Feasibility check | Wiring-sanity on a 256-step single-band sinusoid in a real execution environment (not performed in this sandbox; explicitly disclaimed). |\\n| Out of scope | Image/video SiT, discrete-token trajectories, wall-clock as primary endpoint, real-world motion benchmarks. |\\n| Sandbox execution | None. Deliverable is a design document. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 13. Item explicitly noted as not answered by the design\\n\\n- No empirical result is provided.\\n- No code is provided (workspace is empty; no execution environment to test in).\\n- No training run is reported or implied.\\n- No prediction is made about which arm will satisfy $H_1$.\\n- The two supplementary topics `neurips2024/93022/6:setup_4` and `…:setup_5` are unified into one design per researcher instruction (i); they are not carried as separate designs in this document.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**End of design document.** Any future execution of this design should treat this document as the pre-registration artefact; deviations (different primary endpoint, different horizon, different seed count, different bootstrap count, different inference protocol, or post-hoc tuning outside a declared tuning budget) should be reported as such and weaken the inference to the extent of the deviation.\",\"evidence\":\"### Repository observations\\n- `/workspace` contains only `task.json` (511 bytes). No SiT, Hyena, or trajectory-modelling code, papers, notes, or references exist locally (verified twice during this session).\\n- No SiT / Hyena / paper / repository / research-themed directory is reachable anywhere up to depth 3; only irrelevant system paths (e.g. `/usr/share/maven-repo`) match.\\n- `$HOME`, `/tmp`, `/data` are empty of relevant material.\\n- Python 3.12.3 with 24 CPU cores available; no GPU (nvidia-smi cannot communicate with a driver); no `torch` installed; network disabled per task spec. Combined with `task.json`'s \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\\\", this confirms the deliverable must be a design document only and no execution is performed in the sandbox.\\n\\n### Locked design items traced to the researcher's resolutions\\n- Time axis = roll-out / temporal axis (resolution a).\\n- Hyena variant = standard Hyena-family (two short convs + multiplicative gating + FFT-implicit long conv); non-causal = bidirectional (resolution b).\\n- Dataset = synthetic multi-timescale (≥3 decades, analytic ground truth) + Lorenz-63; train-short / eval-long (resolution c).\\n- Length ladder = 64 / 256 / 1024 / 4096, identical across arms (resolution d).\\n- Inference = chunked two-pass rollout with fixed future-window substitution rule, identical across arms (resolution e).\\n- Matched FLOPs as cost control; #params and activation memory reported separately; wall-clock not claimed (resolution f).\\n- Primary endpoint = summed log-PSD error at $T=4096$ across 3 decade bands; secondary endpoints as proposed (resolution g).\\n- Stats = minimum 3 seeds paired by seed, paired bootstrap 95% CIs with $B_\\\\text{boot}=10{,}000$, pre-registered before observation (resolution h).\\n- Single unified design with two arms; optional causal-Hyena sub-arm as secondary, not primary (resolution i).\\n- Continuous-valued sequence elements (resolution j).\\n- Feasibility = wiring-sanity plan with explicit caveat that it is not a reported result (resolution k).\\n- Controls explicitly enumerated (resolution k).\\n\\n### Formulas locked in §7\\n- $L_{\\\\text{primary}}(T=4096) = \\\\sum_b \\\\frac{1}{|B_b|} \\\\sum_{f\\\\in B_b} (\\\\log\\\\hat P(f) - \\\\log P^\\\\*(f))^2$ across 3 decade bands.\\n- RMSE($h$), NLL($h$), AC-MSE($\\\\mathcal{L}$), Wasserstein-1 on increments, training-stability scalars, FLOPs/cost curve as secondary.\\n- Paired bootstrap CI on $\\\\bar d = \\\\overline{L_\\\\text{primary}(B) - L_\\\\text{primary}(A)}$ at $T=4096$, $B_\\\\text{boot}=10{,}000$, 95% percentile CI.\\n\\n### Disclaimers\\n- No experiment is claimed to have been executed.\\n- Workspace inspection confirms there is nothing local to draw on; design is parameterised from public-source definitions and the researcher's resolutions.\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"[Risk acknowledged via §10] The design does not include a held-out distinct benchmark beyond synthetic and Lorenz-63; the implicit scope of any \\\"long, consistent trajectories\\\" claim is limited to those two settings.</item>\\n\",\"$text\":\"[Design choice, defensible] When the model head is deterministic, NLL is reported as a Gaussian-fit residual NLL with a fixed isotropic variance estimate, clearly labelled as a degradation of the deterministic case.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"[Design choice, defensible] Sampling procedure (solver + NFE) and noise schedule are fixed across arms; the design's pre-registration does not depend on which solver or which NFE is chosen, only that both are matched.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"[Design choice, defensible] Welch-window length and 50%-overlap are fixed across arms and reported; the design does not require optimal Welch parameters, only matched parameters across arms.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"[Design choice, defensible] PSD estimator defaults to Welch with fixed window length and 50% overlap; parameters held constant across arms; Lorenz-63 uses the same estimator applied to held-out fine-integration ground truth rather than analytic PSD.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"[Design choice, defensible] The chunk size C=128 and future-window W=64 for the inference protocol are placeholders to be locked at wiring-sanity time; the design's conclusions hold as long as both arms use the same (C, W).</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"[Locked from researcher resolution k] Wiring-sanity = a tiny 256-step single-band sinusoid smoke run in a real execution environment, listed as a plan only and explicitly not a reported result; explicit caveats accompany any later execution.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"[Locked from researcher resolution j] Sequence elements are continuous-valued; discrete-token considerations (e.g. HyenaDNA-style discrete outputs) are out of scope.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"[Locked from researcher resolution i] setup_4 and setup_5 are unified into a single design with two primary arms (Attention baseline, Hyena treatment); an optional causal-Hyena sub-arm is included as a clearly-labelled secondary analysis and not pre-registered as a primary endpoint.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"[Locked from researcher resolution h] Statistical analysis = minimum 3 seeds per arm per dataset, paired by seed, paired bootstrap 95% CIs on the primary endpoint, pre-registered before any (hypothetical) observation; bootstrap resample count = 10000.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"[Locked from researcher resolution g] Primary endpoint = summed log-PSD error at T=4096 across 3 decade bands (short / medium / long); secondary endpoints = forecast RMSE / NLL at horizons, autocorrelation consistency at multiple lags, Wasserstein-1 on increments, training stability scalars, FLOPs / activation cost curve.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"[Locked from researcher resolution f] Cost control = matched FLOPs at forward-pass level referenced to the training sequence length; parameter count and activation memory reported separately; wall-clock not claimed as part of the result.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"[Locked from researcher resolution e] Inference protocol = chunked two-pass rollout with a fixed future-window substitution rule, applied identically across arms (Arm A, Arm B, and any sub-arm), with the same buffer length W and chunk length C.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"[Locked from researcher resolution d] Length ladder = 64 / 256 / 1024 / 4096, evaluated identically across arms.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"[Locked from researcher resolution c] Datasets = synthetic multi-timescale sequences (≥3 decades of period, analytic PSD / autocorrelation / phase) plus Lorenz-63 (held-out fine-integration ground truth), evaluated in a train-short / eval-long regime.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"[Locked from researcher resolution b] Hyena operator = standard family: two short convolutions + element-wise multiplicative gating + implicit long convolution via FFT, instantiated bidirectionally; specific kernel sizes and gating parameterisations are placeholders to be locked at wiring-sanity time and the design's conclusions do not depend on a specific choice within that family.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"[Locked from researcher resolution a] Time axis = roll-out / temporal axis of the generated trajectory, not diffusion-time attention inside SiT.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"[FIXED REQUIREMENT, not assumption] Trajectories must be long (operationalised as the 4096-step ladder step) and must span multiple timescales (operationalised as ≥3 decade bands).</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"[FIXED REQUIREMENT, not assumption] SiT's time-axis operator is replaced by a non-causal Hyena-family operator; remaining architecture is unchanged.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"[FIXED REQUIREMENT, not assumption] Deliverable is a design document only; no claim of execution is made.</item>\\n\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"[Unresolvable locally] Any future execution must run in a real execution environment with a GPU; the present sandbox has none. The wiring-sanity plan in §9 is a future step only and is not performed here.</item>\\n\",\"$text\":\"[Open] The specific embedding dimension, block count, and attention / operator width are not yet locked; they must be chosen together with the matched-FLOPs recipe at design-finalisation time. The design's shape holds for any compatible choice.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"[Resolved by researcher resolution i] The relationship between setup_4 and setup_5 is unified here into one design; if the researcher later prefers to carry them as two separate designs, the primary endpoint, dataset, ladder, protocol, and statistical plan above can be reused without change.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"[Open, defensibly held as risk in §10] Whether the longer-horizon structural metrics (long-band log-PSD, AC-MSE at lag 512) are stable across PSD-estimator settings; a sensitivity check (Welch vs. Bartlett) is listed as part of the residual-risk mitigation.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"[Open, defensibly held as risk in §10] Whether the minimum seed count (3) yields a bootstrap CI narrow enough to detect the effect size of interest; the design documents this explicitly and treats its absence as a residual risk rather than as a hidden correction.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"[Open, defensibly held as risk in §10] Whether the synthetic generator's analytic ground truth is itself a fair test (e.g. enough phase variety, enough slow-component complexity) to expose a Hyena-attention difference.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"[Open, defensibly held as risk in §10] Whether the chunked two-pass rollout protocol is genuinely symmetric between Attention and Hyena, especially around causal-mask conventions inside a buffered span.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"[Open, defensibly held as risk in §10] Whether the matched-FLOPs recipe can be implemented without silently changing another architectural component that drives any observed effect.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"[Resolved by researcher but worth restating] Whether the implicit long convolution in Hyena here uses the original Hyena implicit-parametrisation or an equivalent (e.g. Hyena-SE); as long as it is bidirectional, the family is fixed and the family level, not the specific kernel-size recipe, drives the conclusion.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"[Resolved by researcher but worth restating] Whether the original SiT uses causal or non-causal time-axis attention as its baseline before substitution; this is recorded as the baseline's actual causality and held fixed in Arm A.</item>\\n\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-453fb61d-7b21-4578-8865-d79e4600f2b1", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 25}
{"id": "b73691a5-4cf3-48f9-a7fc-5f7eb00f5e04", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T14:29:09.813300", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "57f02c8d-4ede-4cc5-b08d-c58dfedd67e3", "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-6ae9ae20-9cd0-45d8-9f6f-a9224531d714", "error": "Error validating tool 'end_researcher_turn': 2 validation errors for ReviewHandoffAction\nassumptions.0\n  Input should be a valid string [type=string_type, input_value={'item': {'item': {'item'...tion is made.</item>\\n'}, input_type=dict]\n    For further information visit https://errors.pydantic.dev/2.13/v/string_type\nuncertainties.0\n  Input should be a valid string [type=string_type, input_value={'item': {'item': {'item'...xed in Arm A.</item>\\n\"}, input_type=dict]\n    For further information visit https://errors.pydantic.dev/2.13/v/string_type. Parameters provided: ['stage', 'answer', 'evidence', 'assumptions', 'uncertainties']", "classification": {"kind": "agent_action", "retryable": true, "user_action": "retry", "error_id": null}, "kind": "AgentErrorEvent", "source_event_index": 26}
{"id": "1da8321a-c53e-4b4a-a0f9-e63d3643d698", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T14:30:32.551020", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "b73691a5-4cf3-48f9-a7fc-5f7eb00f5e04", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "## Experiment design -- SiT with non-causal Hyena time operator for long, multi-timescale trajectories\n\nThis is a design document only. No experiment is executed in this sandbox (no GPU, no Torch, no network). The single execution-grade item is a wiring-sanity plan listed in section 9, with an explicit caveat that it is not a reported result.\n\nCentral question (locked): does replacing the time-axis attention mechanism with a non-causal Hyena operator in SiT allow the model to generate extremely long, consistent trajectories that capture dynamics over multiple timescales.\n\nSection 0 separates fixed task requirements from design assumptions. Sections 1 to 10 cover, in order: hypothesis and rationale; treatment structure; controlled variables; inference protocol for non-causality; datasets and ground truth; evaluation ladder and protocol; endpoints with exact formulas; statistical analysis plan; feasibility checks; residual risks and invalidation conditions.\n\n### 0. Fixed requirements vs design assumptions\n\nFixed task requirements (not negotiable here):\n- replace the SiT time-axis operator with a non-causal Hyena-family operator\n- compare against SiT with its original time-axis attention\n- trajectories must be long and span multiple timescales\n- consistency applies to the generated trajectory itself\n- deliverable is a design document (no claim of an executed run)\n- task.json states design-only and that repositories and compute are not required\n\nDesign assumptions (locked from researcher resolutions a through k):\n- time axis = roll-out / temporal axis of the generated trajectory, not diffusion time\n- Hyena variant = standard Hyena-family (two short convolutions + element-wise multiplicative gating + implicit long convolution via FFT), instantiated bidirectionally\n- datasets = synthetic multi-timescale sequences with at least three decades of characteristic period plus Lorenz-63\n- length ladder = 64 / 256 / 1024 / 4096, evaluated identically across arms\n- inference = chunked two-pass rollout with a fixed future-window substitution rule, applied identically across arms\n- cost control = matched FLOPs; parameter count and activation memory reported separately; wall-clock not claimed\n- primary endpoint = pre-registered scalar summed log-PSD error at the longest horizon across three timescale bands\n- statistics = at least three random seeds per arm, paired by seed, paired bootstrap 95 percent CIs pre-registered before any hypothetical observation\n- unified design with two primary arms; an optional causal-Hyena sub-arm is included as clearly secondary, not primary\n- sequence elements are continuous-valued\n\n### 1. Hypothesis and rationale\n\nWorking hypothesis (informal): a non-causal Hyena operator, by allowing the model to read both past and future along the roll-out axis within a buffered window during training and inference, can preserve low-frequency (long-timescale) structure of the underlying dynamics further into the horizon than causal or unidirectional time attention, without sacrificing high-frequency fidelity, and at matched FLOPs.\n\nFormal pre-registered hypothesis. Let L_primary(T; arm) be the primary endpoint (summed log-PSD error at horizon T; see section 7) computed for arm in {A, B} at T = 4096. The pre-registered alternative is a paired cross-seed comparison at T = 4096:\n- H0: E[L_primary(4096; B)] = E[L_primary(4096; A)]\n- H1 (directional, pre-specified): E[L_primary(4096; B)] less than E[L_primary(4096; A)]\n\nA two-tailed 95 percent paired-bootstrap CI on L_primary(B) minus L_primary(A) is reported. Rejection of H0 requires the CI to exclude 0 with the directional sign of H1.\n\nWhy this is primary: log-domain PSD is the most directly interpretable diagnostic of multi-timescale fidelity; each decade of period contributes a distinct band; the log-domain MSE penalises order-of-magnitude distortions that linear RMSE would dilute. The longest horizon (4096) is where the failure mode -- loss of long-timescale coherence -- should manifest most strongly if it exists, and where the cost gap between attention and Hyena should be visible.\n\nWhy the treatment might help: bidirectional neighbourhood context lets the operator resolve local ambiguity using immediate neighbours in a way that strictly causal attention must recover across many steps, where compounding error depresses long-timescale coherence.\n\nWhy it might not: the future-window protocol at inference is shared by both arms, so any buffering-related advantage is symmetric; local fidelity may trade off against long-horizon gain; the bottleneck may lie outside the time operator.\n\n### 2. Treatment structure\n\nTwo primary arms:\n- Arm A -- SiT-TimeAttn (baseline). SiT with its original time-axis attention. Causality flag is whatever the original SiT specification uses (typically causal-masked); that flag is recorded and held fixed for Arm A. All other components identical to Arm B.\n- Arm B -- SiT-Hyena (treatment). SiT with the time-axis attention replaced by the Hyena-family operator defined below. Non-causal: bidirectional along the time axis.\n\nHyena time operator exact specification:\n1. Two short 1-D convolutions along the time axis, suggested kernel sizes 3 and 3, producing intermediate features u and v.\n2. Element-wise multiplicative gate g = sigma(Linear(u)) multiplied element-wise by v, with sigma as a configurable nonlinearity (default GELU).\n3. Implicit long convolution via FFT: project the gated signal with an implicitly parameterised long kernel, multiply by FFT, apply inverse FFT. Kernel parameterisation follows Hyena-family practice (e.g., two short MLPs parametrising a small set of values that are then interpolated or windowed to length T).\n4. Non-causal: no future masking; symmetric padding for the short convolutions; the FFT long convolution is naturally non-causal.\n\nThese defaults are placeholders to be locked at wiring-sanity time. The design's conclusions hold for any architectural choice within the Hyena family.\n\nOptional secondary sub-arm (clearly labelled, not a primary endpoint):\n- Arm B-prime -- SiT-Hyena-Causal. Same as B with a strictly causal Hyena (asymmetric padding on the short convolutions and a one-sided modification of the FFT long convolution). This arm separates \"Hyena vs attention\" from \"non-causality vs causality\" so that any Hyena-arm gain in the primary endpoint can be attributed correctly. It is reported as a clearly secondary analysis and is not pre-registered as a primary endpoint.\n\nWhat is not an arm: a causal-attention sub-arm is not required for the question and is left out of the unified design; the omission is recorded in section 10.\n\n### 3. Controlled variables (held fixed across both arms)\n\nThe following are explicitly held constant between Arm A and Arm B (and Arm B-prime unless flagged).\n\nArchitecture outside the time operator: spatial mixing block (operator, depth, width); cross-axis / conditioning pathway including class, text, diffusion-time conditioning as in source; token / patch / positional embeddings including rotary / ALiBi / sinusoidal; normalisation placement and type; output head; number of SiT blocks; embedding dimension; attention / operator width.\n\nOptimisation and schedule: optimiser AdamW with beta1 0.9, beta2 0.95, fixed weight decay; learning-rate schedule (peak value, warmup length, decay shape); total training tokens; total optimiser steps; batch size; gradient accumulation; mixed-precision policy; gradient-clipping value; dropout rates; random seeds for data sampling, parameter init, dropout.\n\nDiffusion and sampling: noise schedule (cosine / linear / flow-matching parameter, fixed once chosen); sampling procedure with the same solver and the same number of function evaluations (NFE) and step-size schedule; identical inference protocol (chunked two-pass rollout with fixed future-window substitution; see section 4) applied identically across arms.\n\nData: preprocessing pipeline (normalisation, channel ordering, clipping); training sequence length held at a single value within the train-short regime; evaluation uses the length ladder; train / validation / test split fixed across arms; held-out ground truth for evaluation (analytic closed-form for the synthetic generator; numerical integration at fine resolution for Lorenz-63).\n\nCost control: matched FLOPs at forward-pass level referenced to the training sequence length; parameter count and activation memory (peak bytes per forward pass) reported separately and not part of the cost-control target; wall-clock is not claimed as an endpoint in this design-only deliverable.\n\nHardware and software: single accelerator target, one GPU type, to be specified at run time; single software stack (framework version, CUDA, library versions); determinism flags where supported.\n\nThe single item explicitly allowed to vary between arms is the time-axis operator. Arm B-prime further varies causal/non-causal masking within the Hyena family.\n\n### 4. Inference protocol for non-causality (applied identically across arms)\n\nChunked two-pass rollout with fixed future-window substitution rule. At generation time, the model produces chunks of length C (suggested default 128), with a fixed future-window size W (suggested default 64) used to buffer the next W predicted steps. Within each chunk, the time-axis operator sees both past context and the buffered future exactly as in training. After producing each chunk, the window is shifted: the first C produced steps are kept as the roll-out, the buffered W future steps are discarded and replaced by the next chunk's predictions.\n\nThe protocol is applied identically in Arms A, B, and B-prime, with the same C and W, the same chunk boundaries, and the same substitution rule. Within a chunk, the Hyena operator runs bidirectionally as designed; the attention baseline performs attention over the same buffered span with the same masking rules as in training. By equating the windowed span between arms, the protocol does not systematically favour one operator.\n\n### 5. Datasets and ground truth\n\n5.1 Synthetic multi-timescale trajectories (primary benchmark).\nGenerator: a deterministic continuous-time signal x(t) constructed as a sum of three or more components spanning at least three decades of characteristic period. Canonical form:\nx(t) = A_s sin(2 pi t / T_s + phi_s) + A_m sin(2 pi t / T_m + phi_m) + x_l(t)\nwhere T_s = 1, T_m = 100, l corresponds to a slow component with characteristic period T_l at least 10^4. Phases are fixed per generated sample to permit phase-coherence metrics. Realisations are produced at a sampling rate commensurate with the shortest component (at least 10 samples per shortest period) and discretised.\n\nGround truth available analytically: closed-form PSD P_x(f) as a sum of delta peaks for sinusoidal components plus an analytic PSD for the slow component; closed-form autocorrelation R_x(tau); closed-form phase structure (instantaneous amplitude and frequency). This is the primary benchmark because every key property in the central question admits an analytic ground truth.\n\n5.2 Lorenz-63 (grounding in physical dynamics).\nGenerator: Lorenz-63 ODE at standard parameters (sigma = 10, rho = 28, beta = 8/3), integrated with a fine-step RK4 (or equivalent) at small step size (e.g. delta t = 0.005 in Lorenz time units), downsampled to the discretisation rate used for evaluation. The (x, y, z) trajectory is treated as a multi-channel signal; PSD and other metrics are computed per channel and averaged.\nGround truth: held-out fine-resolution numerical integration (Lorenz has no closed-form PSD). The horizon ladder uses integration-time units commensurate with the chosen discretisation rate. Lorenz-63 is included because its multi-timescale structure (fast orbital timescale vs slow switching between lobes) is well-studied and physically interpretable.\n\n5.3 Across datasets. Multiple timescales is operationalised as at least three decade-separated period bands (short, medium, long). Extremely long is operationalised as the 4096-step evaluation horizon in section 6, with shorter horizons retained for diagnostic purposes.\n\n5.4 Out of scope. Image-video SiT (UCF-101 / Kinetics long-horizon video); long-horizon language / discrete-token trajectories; real-world motion-capture benchmarks. The unified design does not run these; extensions are noted but not part of this design.\n\n### 6. Evaluation ladder and protocol\n\nSequence lengths: 64 / 256 / 1024 / 4096 steps. Each length is evaluated identically in both arms with the same protocol (section 4) and the same chunk size C and future window W. Training length is held fixed within the train-short regime and reused across arms.\n\nFor each (length, seed) pair the protocol produces one generated roll-out, pointwise deterministic forecast skill versus held-out ground truth, spectral estimates and band-decomposed errors, and cost figures at forward-pass level for the chosen length.\n\nLength varies as the ladder dimension (64, 256, 1024, 4096). Seed varies as the randomness dimension (at least 3 seeds per arm per length per dataset). The primary endpoint commits to T = 4096; the ladder exists for transparency and reproducibility, including locating any cross-over length at which the relative ordering of A and B reverses.\n\n### 7. Endpoints with exact formulas\n\nNotation. x-hat = (x-hat[1], ..., x-hat[T]) is a single generated trajectory; x-star = (x-star[1], ..., x-star[T]) is the corresponding ground-truth trajectory. For multi-channel data (Lorenz), endpoints are computed per channel and averaged. Welch PSD with fixed window length W_welch (distinct from the future-window W) and 50 percent overlap is the default estimator; parameters are fixed across arms.\n\nPre-registration: the endpoints, the log-PSD convention, the band partition, the forecast horizons, and the set of autocorrelation lags are written down before any data is examined and locked in this design document.\n\n7.1 Primary endpoint.\n\nL_primary(T = 4096) = sum over bands b in {short, medium, long} of (1 / |B_b|) times sum over f in B_b of (log P-hat(f) - log P-star(f))^2\n\nwhere P-hat(f) is the PSD estimator of x-hat, P-star(f) is the ground-truth PSD (analytic for synthetic; estimator of held-out fine integration for Lorenz), and {B_b} is a fixed partition of the frequency axis into three bands aligned to the period decades of the generator (e.g. for synthetic, B_short = {f : f in [f_s/2, 2 f_s]}, B_medium = {f : f in [f_m/2, 2 f_m]}, B_long = {f : f in [1/(2T), f_l/2]}, with f_s, f_m, f_l derived from T_s, T_m, T_l). The log-domain MSE in each band is summed across bands. Lower is better.\n\n7.2 Secondary endpoints.\n\nForecast RMSE and NLL at multiple horizons. H is a fixed subset of {16, 64, 256, 1024}, evaluated during roll-out (avoiding T itself so the endpoint is on the trajectory, not at the end-point only).\n\nRMSE(h) = sqrt( (1 / (N_s N_h)) sum_{s=1..N_s} sum_{t : t+h in [1,T]} (x-hat^{(s)}[t+h] - x-star^{(s)}[t+h])^2 )\n\nNLL(h) = -(1 / (N_s N_h)) sum_{s,t} log p_theta_s( x-hat^{(s)}[t+h] | x-hat^{(s)}[<= t] )\n\nwith the predictive density p_theta parameterised by the model head at training time; if the head is deterministic, NLL is replaced by a Gaussian-fit residual NLL as a clearly-labelled degradation.\n\nAutocorrelation consistency at multiple lags. L-set subset of {1, 8, 64, 512}.\n\nAC_tau(x) = sum_{t=1..T-tau} (x[t] - x-bar)(x[t+tau] - x-bar) / sum_{t=1..T} (x[t] - x-bar)^2\n\nEndpoint is AC-MSE = mean over tau in L-set of (AC_tau(x-hat) - AC_tau(x-star))^2, per seed, then averaged.\n\nWasserstein-1 on increments. Distribution of Delta_t x-hat = x-hat[t+1] - x-hat[t] vs Delta_t x-star. Reported as W_1 using the empirical one-dimensional Wasserstein-1 with linear interpolation. Sensitive to distributional drift; complementary to AC-MSE.\n\nTraining stability. Per training run: (i) maximum and final values of the gradient norm; (ii) loss-curve shape (smoothed training loss at fixed iteration milestones); (iii) NaN / Inf event count; (iv) effective rank of the penultimate-block activations on a held-out batch (mean across a small calibration set). Each is a scalar reported per seed.\n\nScale of cost curve. Analytical estimate of forward FLOPs as a function of T in {64, 256, 1024, 4096} for each arm, and empirical activation-memory estimate at the training sequence length, reported on the same axes as supporting evidence, not as components of the cost-control target.\n\nThe secondary endpoints do not affect acceptance or rejection of H0; they support interpretation of the primary result.\n\n7.3 What is intentionally not an endpoint in this design.\n\nWall-clock training time (no GPU / no execution; wall-clock not claimed); qualitative perceptual scores for held-out humans (out of scope); any metric on a held-out distinct dataset (e.g. Internet video) -- dataset choice is locked to synthetic plus Lorenz.\n\n### 8. Statistical analysis plan\n\nPre-registration. The primary endpoint, the directional alternative H1, the seed count, the bootstrap resample count, and the bootstrap CI definition are locked in this design document before any execution. No test is performed in the present sandbox; pre-registration here applies to any future execution of this design.\n\nSeeds. Minimum N_seeds = 3 per arm, per dataset, per evaluation length, paired by seed (seed s for Arm A and seed s for Arm B share their data-sampling seed and differ only in operator). Increasing N_seeds is encouraged if budget allows; the minimum is the smallest count that yields a paired bootstrap CI with practical width. The seed count is part of pre-registration.\n\nPaired comparison. For each arm, theta in {A, B}, compute ell_s^{(theta)} = L_primary^{(s)}(theta) at T = 4096, where s indexes the seed. The paired difference is d_s = ell_s^{(B)} - ell_s^{(A)}. The summary statistic of interest is d-bar (mean difference).\n\nBootstrap CI. Resample seed index with replacement B_boot = 10000 times; for each resample compute d-bar-star. The 95 percent CI is the percentile interval (d-bar-star at 0.025, d-bar-star at 0.975). Reject H0 in favour of the directional H1 iff the entire CI lies below zero. Otherwise, report the CI and the point estimate.\n\nMultiplicity. The ladder produces L_primary at four lengths; only T = 4096 is the pre-registered primary. Shorter lengths are reported as the secondary diagnostic L_64, L_256, L_1024 but no multiplicity correction is pre-applied and they are not used for acceptance or rejection.\n\nSecondary endpoints. Reported with the same paired-bootstrap structure, sign of effect recorded but no rejection criterion pre-registered. Band-level decomposition of the primary endpoint is reported as a decomposition, not as separate endpoints.\n\nEffect-size reporting. Always report point estimate, 95 percent CI, and the matched-FLOPs configuration under which it was obtained. Do not report p-values as the primary inferential quantity; the bootstrap CI is the inferential quantity.\n\nMissing-data handling. If a seed's run fails (NaN, divergence), the seed is replaced by another seed up to a maximum of N_seeds + 2 runs per arm; failed runs are recorded and reported.\n\n### 9. Feasibility checks\n\nWiring-sanity plan (the only execution-grade check that could in principle be performed outside this sandbox). A small smoke run, not a result of the study, would be performed in a real execution environment to confirm that both arms are wired correctly before any production training begins. It is listed as a plan, not a result. The actual check is not performed in this sandbox; this design-only deliverable does not claim any run succeeded.\n\nSanity data: a tiny trajectory of length T_train = 256, generated by a single-band sinusoid plus small noise, with analytic ground truth.\nSanity schedule: very short training (a few hundred optimiser steps), small batch, no scheduling tricks; identical hyperparameters across arms except the time operator.\nSanity check: after training, the model is asked to denoise a freshly initialised noisy real trajectory and produce a qualitative plot. Expected behaviour: both arms reproduce the sinusoid shape with phase approximately preserved and amplitude close to the true value, up to under-training tolerance. Failure looks like a flat output, a constant mean, a phase-shift inversion, or a divergent curve.\nWhat this does not establish: wiring-sanity does not establish that the primary endpoint shows a treatment difference, nor that the result generalises beyond the sanity-task distribution.\nNo run is reported here: this deliverable is the design; no execution is claimed or implied.\n\nDesign-side feasibility checks (those that can be performed in the present sandbox, no execution of the experiment):\n- workspace inspection confirmed empty of SiT/Hyena materials (see evidence)\n- environment inspection confirmed Python 3.12.3 with 24 CPUs, no GPU, no Torch, no network\n- pre-registration mechanics: each formula and each pre-registered step (seed count, bootstrap resample count, endpoint definition, ladder, dataset, protocol) is stated in this document and reproducible from sections 6 to 8 alone\n\n### 10. Residual risks and conditions that would invalidate the design's conclusions\n\nThe conclusions of any future run of this design would be invalidated under any of the following conditions.\n\n1. Matched-FLOPs material-change risk. Setting matched FLOPs exactly equal forces width/depth trade-offs that could themselves drive any observed difference. Conclusion invalidated if the recipe silently changes another component. Mitigation: report and constrain depth / width bounds so the recipe cannot reach a degenerate configuration; include a sensitivity over at least one alternative matched-FLOPs recipe.\n\n2. Inference-protocol favouritism risk. The chunked two-pass rollout gives the buffered future to both arms. If buffering interacts unexpectedly with one operator but not the other, the protocol could favour one arm non-trivially. Conclusion invalidated if the protocol's effects are not symmetric. Mitigation: verify symmetry by an ablation of buffer size W in {0, 32, 64, 128}; if attention's relative performance differs strongly across W but Hyena's does not, revisit the protocol.\n\n3. Synthetic-saturation risk. If both arms reach essentially the analytic ground truth on the synthetic dataset by T = 1024, the experiment may fail to discriminate at T = 4096. Conclusion invalidated only if the failure is silent. Mitigation: report band-decomposed decomposition at all ladder steps; report a noise-robustness stress test (PSNR of generated trajectories under added input noise).\n\n4. Lorenz-63 idiosyncrasy risk. The fast/slow separation in Lorenz is specific, so behaviour on it may not generalise to other physical systems. Conclusion invalidated if the design's claim is interpreted too broadly. Mitigation: report band definitions and per-band decomposition; document limits of inference in the discussion.\n\n5. PSD-estimator bias risk at long horizon. Welch on T = 4096 with default settings biases the low-frequency band toward higher variance than the analytic formula; bias is unstable across seeds. Conclusion on the long band could be unreliable even if the headline number is statistically significant elsewhere. Mitigation: enforce a fixed Welch configuration across arms, report the band-level L_b values alongside the sum, and require the long-band effect to be consistent in sign across at least two PSD-estimator settings (Welch and Bartlett).\n\n6. Low-seed detection-power risk. N_seeds = 3 with bootstrap CIs produces wide intervals; the design may fail to detect a real effect (false negative). Conclusion of non-inferiority would be weakened; conclusion of superiority would be unwarranted. Mitigation: report effect size and CI width explicitly; do not infer \"no effect\" from an underpowered comparison.\n\n7. Optional causal sub-arm risk. Arm B-prime uses a non-standard causal mask on the FFT long convolution. If incorrectly implemented, the sub-arm's interpretation collapses. Conclusion of the secondary causal-vs-non-causal decomposition would be invalidated. Mitigation: Arm B-prime is explicitly labelled secondary; its failure does not invalidate the primary arm comparison.\n\n8. Pre-registration scope risk. The pre-registration locks the primary endpoint, the primary horizon, the seed count, and the bootstrap definition. It does not lock corrections for restarts, hyperparameter re-tuning, or non-seed-conditioned learning-rate re-tuning. If post-hoc tuning is allowed, the design's type-I error is uncontrolled. Mitigation: declare a tuning budget (e.g. 10 percent of compute reserved for tuning, not in reported results) at design time.\n\n9. Wall-clock absence risk. Because wall-clock is not claimed in this design-only deliverable, claims about practical efficiency are out of scope. Any extension to claim wall-clock benefits would require a separate, environment-specific design. Conclusion invalidated if claimed. Mitigation: report FLOPs and activation memory analytically (no execution); mark any extension as out of scope.\n\n10. Concept-of-consistent ambiguity risk. \"Consistent\" can be read as trajectory-level fidelity, distributional fidelity, or self-consistency across the roll-out (no drift, no artefacts, no mode collapse). The primary endpoint addresses fidelity (point and spectral); distributional self-consistency is addressed only by Wasserstein-1 and marginal moments. Conclusion invalidated if \"consistent\" is interpreted in a sense not covered by the metrics. Mitigation: report long-run moments of x-hat (drift, variance, kurtosis) and a low-order Wasserstein-1 on the empirical marginal distribution as clearly-labelled supporting evidence.\n\n11. Sandbox-execution impossibility risk. No part of training, sampling, evaluation, or wiring-sanity has been executed in the present sandbox; any claim that the design's predictions were confirmed cannot be made from this deliverable. Mitigation: this design document explicitly disclaims execution.\n\n### 11. Repository observations (evidence)\n\n- /workspace contains only task.json (511 bytes), which restates the question and the note track.md = \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\". No SiT, Hyena, or trajectory-modelling references exist locally.\n- $HOME, /tmp, /data are empty of any relevant material.\n- No topical directory (SiT / Hyena / paper / repo) is reachable anywhere up to depth 3.\n- Python 3.12.3 with 24 CPU cores available; no GPU (nvidia-smi cannot communicate with a driver); no Torch installed; network disabled per task spec.\n- These observations are consistent with task.json and with the design-only nature of the deliverable.\n\n### 12. Summary of the locked design in one screen\n\nArms: A -- SiT-TimeAttn baseline; B -- SiT-Hyena non-causal treatment. Optional sub-arm: B-prime -- SiT-Hyena-Causal (secondary).\n\nDatasets: synthetic multi-timescale (analytic ground truth, at least three decade bands); Lorenz-63 (held-out fine-integration ground truth).\n\nSequence elements: continuous-valued.\n\nTime axis: roll-out / temporal axis.\n\nLength ladder: 64 / 256 / 1024 / 4096, identical across arms.\n\nTraining and eval regime: train-short, eval-long.\n\nInference protocol: chunked two-pass rollout with fixed future-window substitution; identical across arms.\n\nCost control: matched FLOPs; parameter count and activation memory reported separately; wall-clock not claimed.\n\nPrimary endpoint: L_primary(T=4096) = sum over bands b of (1/|B_b|) times sum over f in B_b of (log P-hat(f) - log P-star(f))^2 across three decade bands.\n\nSecondary endpoints: forecast RMSE and NLL at horizons 16, 64, 256, 1024; AC-MSE at lags 1, 8, 64, 512; Wasserstein-1 on increments; training-stability scalars; FLOPs and activation cost curve.\n\nPre-registration: locked at design time -- primary endpoint, primary horizon, seed count, bootstrap count, CI definition.\n\nStatistical test: paired bootstrap CI on L_primary(B) - L_primary(A) at T = 4096, 95 percent CI, B_boot = 10000, at least 3 seeds paired by seed.\n\nHypotheses: H0 equal; H1 Hyena lower one-sided; CI-excludes-0 criterion.\n\nControls (held fixed): spatial mixing, conditioning, embeddings, optimiser, schedule, batch, seed, noise schedule, sampling, NFE, solver, preprocessing, hardware, software, training length.\n\nFeasibility check: wiring-sanity on a 256-step single-band sinusoid in a real execution environment (not performed in this sandbox; explicitly disclaimed).\n\nOut of scope: image or video SiT, discrete-token trajectories, wall-clock as primary endpoint, real-world motion benchmarks.\n\nSandbox execution: none. Deliverable is a design document.\n\n### 13. Items explicitly not answered by the design\n\n- no empirical result is provided\n- no code is provided; the workspace is empty and there is no execution environment\n- no training run is reported or implied\n- no prediction is made about which arm will satisfy H1\n- the two supplementary topics setup_4 and setup_5 are unified into one design per researcher instruction i and are not carried as separate designs\n\nEnd of design document. Any future execution of this design should treat this document as the pre-registration artefact; deviations (different primary endpoint, different horizon, different seed count, different bootstrap count, different inference protocol, or post-hoc tuning outside a declared tuning budget) should be reported as such and weaken the inference to the extent of the deviation.", "evidence": "Workspace inspection: /workspace contains only task.json (511 bytes); no SiT, Hyena, or trajectory-modelling code, papers, notes, or references exist locally (verified at both inspection and start of result drafting). No topical directory is reachable anywhere up to depth 3; only irrelevant system paths (e.g. /usr/share/maven-repo) match. $HOME, /tmp, /data are empty of relevant material.\nEnvironment inspection: Python 3.12.3 with 24 CPU cores available; no GPU (nvidia-smi cannot communicate with a driver); no torch installed; network disabled per task spec.\nCombined with task.json statement that the task is design-only and that repositories/compute are not required, these observations confirm the deliverable must be a design document only and no execution is performed in the sandbox.\nLocked design items traced to the researcher's resolutions: time axis = roll-out / temporal axis (resolution a); Hyena variant = standard Hyena-family with FFT long convolution and non-causal instantiation (resolution b); dataset = synthetic multi-timescale plus Lorenz-63 in train-short / eval-long regime (resolution c); length ladder = 64/256/1024/4096 identical across arms (resolution d); inference = chunked two-pass rollout with fixed future-window substitution applied identically (resolution e); matched FLOPs as cost control with params and activation memory reported separately and wall-clock not claimed (resolution f); primary endpoint = summed log-PSD error at T=4096 across three decade bands with secondary endpoints as proposed (resolution g); statistics = minimum 3 seeds paired by seed, paired bootstrap 95 percent CIs with B_boot=10000, pre-registered (resolution h); single unified design with two primary arms and an optional causal-Hyena sub-arm as secondary (resolution i); continuous-valued sequence elements (resolution j); wiring-sanity feasibility plan with explicit caveat (resolution k); controls explicitly enumerated (resolution k).\nFormulas locked in section 7 of the document: primary endpoint formula, RMSE/NLL at horizons, autocorrelation consistency MSE, Wasserstein-1 on increments, training-stability scalars, FLOPs and memory cost curve.\nDisclaimer: no experiment is claimed to have been executed.", "assumptions": ["[FIXED REQUIREMENT] Deliverable is a design document only; no claim of execution is made.", "[FIXED REQUIREMENT] SiT time-axis operator is replaced by a non-causal Hyena-family operator; remaining architecture unchanged.", "[FIXED REQUIREMENT] Trajectories must be long (operationalised as the 4096-step ladder step) and span multiple timescales (at least three decade bands).", "[Locked, resolution a] Time axis = roll-out / temporal axis of the generated trajectory, not diffusion time.", "[Locked, resolution b] Hyena operator = standard family: two short convolutions + multiplicative gating + FFT-implicit long convolution, instantiated bidirectionally; specific kernel sizes and gating parameterisations are placeholders to be locked at wiring-sanity time.", "[Locked, resolution c] Datasets = synthetic multi-timescale sequences (at least three decades of period, analytic ground truth) plus Lorenz-63 (held-out fine-integration ground truth), in a train-short / eval-long regime.", "[Locked, resolution d] Length ladder = 64 / 256 / 1024 / 4096, evaluated identically across arms.", "[Locked, resolution e] Inference protocol = chunked two-pass rollout with fixed future-window substitution, applied identically across arms with the same C and W.", "[Locked, resolution f] Cost control = matched FLOPs at forward-pass level; parameter count and activation memory reported separately; wall-clock not claimed.", "[Locked, resolution g] Primary endpoint = summed log-PSD error at T=4096 across three decade bands; secondary endpoints as in section 7.2.", "[Locked, resolution h] Statistical analysis = minimum 3 seeds per arm per dataset, paired by seed, paired bootstrap 95 percent CIs on the primary endpoint, pre-registered before any hypothetical observation; B_boot = 10000.", "[Locked, resolution i] setup_4 and setup_5 unified into one design with two primary arms; causal-Hyena sub-arm included as clearly secondary, not primary.", "[Locked, resolution j] Sequence elements are continuous-valued; discrete-token Hyena considerations are out of scope.", "[Locked, resolution k] Wiring-sanity = tiny 256-step single-band sinusoid smoke run in a real execution environment, listed as a plan only and explicitly not a reported result.", "[Design choice] Chunk size C = 128 and future window W = 64 are placeholders to be locked at wiring-sanity time; design conclusions hold as long as both arms use the same C and W.", "[Design choice] Welch-window length and 50 percent overlap are fixed across arms; design does not require optimal Welch parameters, only matched parameters.", "[Design choice] Sampling procedure (solver plus NFE) and noise schedule held constant across arms; pre-registration does not depend on which solver or NFE is chosen, only that both are matched.", "[Design choice] When the model head is deterministic, NLL is reported as a Gaussian-fit residual NLL with a fixed isotropic variance estimate, clearly labelled.", "[Risk acknowledged, section 10] Design does not include a held-out distinct benchmark beyond synthetic and Lorenz-63; scope of any claim is limited to those two settings."], "uncertainties": ["[Unresolvable locally] Any future execution must run in a real execution environment with a GPU; the present sandbox has none. Wiring-sanity in section 9 is a future step only and is not performed here.", "[Open] Embedding dimension, block count, attention / operator width are not yet locked; must be chosen with the matched-FLOPs recipe at design finalisation. Design shape holds for any compatible choice.", "[Resolved per resolution i] setup_4 and setup_5 unified into one design here; if the researcher later prefers two separate designs, the primary endpoint, dataset, ladder, protocol, and statistical plan above are reusable without change.", "[Open, risk in section 10] Whether longer-horizon structural metrics (long-band log-PSD, AC-MSE at lag 512) are stable across PSD-estimator settings; Welch-vs-Bartlett sensitivity is listed as mitigation.", "[Open, risk in section 10] Whether minimum seed count (3) yields a bootstrap CI narrow enough to detect the effect size of interest; explicitly documented as a residual risk.", "[Open, risk in section 10] Whether the synthetic generator's analytic ground truth is itself a fair test (sufficient phase variety, sufficient slow-component complexity) to expose a Hyena-attention difference.", "[Open, risk in section 10] Whether the chunked two-pass rollout protocol is genuinely symmetric between Attention and Hyena, especially around causal-mask conventions inside a buffered span.", "[Open, risk in section 10] Whether the matched-FLOPs recipe can be implemented without silently changing another architectural component that drives any observed effect.", "[Resolved but noted] Whether original SiT uses causal or non-causal time-axis attention as its baseline; recorded as baseline's actual causality and held fixed in Arm A.", "[Resolved but noted] Whether the Hyena FFT-implicit long convolution uses the original Hyena implicit-parametrisation or an equivalent such as Hyena-SE; family level, not specific kernel-size recipe, drives the conclusion."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-2877ea06-3102-428b-b02f-d95d45d43d2f", "tool_call": {"id": "call-2877ea06-3102-428b-b02f-d95d45d43d2f", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Retry with shorter string items\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"## Experiment design -- SiT with non-causal Hyena time operator for long, multi-timescale trajectories\\n\\nThis is a design document only. No experiment is executed in this sandbox (no GPU, no Torch, no network). The single execution-grade item is a wiring-sanity plan listed in section 9, with an explicit caveat that it is not a reported result.\\n\\nCentral question (locked): does replacing the time-axis attention mechanism with a non-causal Hyena operator in SiT allow the model to generate extremely long, consistent trajectories that capture dynamics over multiple timescales.\\n\\nSection 0 separates fixed task requirements from design assumptions. Sections 1 to 10 cover, in order: hypothesis and rationale; treatment structure; controlled variables; inference protocol for non-causality; datasets and ground truth; evaluation ladder and protocol; endpoints with exact formulas; statistical analysis plan; feasibility checks; residual risks and invalidation conditions.\\n\\n### 0. Fixed requirements vs design assumptions\\n\\nFixed task requirements (not negotiable here):\\n- replace the SiT time-axis operator with a non-causal Hyena-family operator\\n- compare against SiT with its original time-axis attention\\n- trajectories must be long and span multiple timescales\\n- consistency applies to the generated trajectory itself\\n- deliverable is a design document (no claim of an executed run)\\n- task.json states design-only and that repositories and compute are not required\\n\\nDesign assumptions (locked from researcher resolutions a through k):\\n- time axis = roll-out / temporal axis of the generated trajectory, not diffusion time\\n- Hyena variant = standard Hyena-family (two short convolutions + element-wise multiplicative gating + implicit long convolution via FFT), instantiated bidirectionally\\n- datasets = synthetic multi-timescale sequences with at least three decades of characteristic period plus Lorenz-63\\n- length ladder = 64 / 256 / 1024 / 4096, evaluated identically across arms\\n- inference = chunked two-pass rollout with a fixed future-window substitution rule, applied identically across arms\\n- cost control = matched FLOPs; parameter count and activation memory reported separately; wall-clock not claimed\\n- primary endpoint = pre-registered scalar summed log-PSD error at the longest horizon across three timescale bands\\n- statistics = at least three random seeds per arm, paired by seed, paired bootstrap 95 percent CIs pre-registered before any hypothetical observation\\n- unified design with two primary arms; an optional causal-Hyena sub-arm is included as clearly secondary, not primary\\n- sequence elements are continuous-valued\\n\\n### 1. Hypothesis and rationale\\n\\nWorking hypothesis (informal): a non-causal Hyena operator, by allowing the model to read both past and future along the roll-out axis within a buffered window during training and inference, can preserve low-frequency (long-timescale) structure of the underlying dynamics further into the horizon than causal or unidirectional time attention, without sacrificing high-frequency fidelity, and at matched FLOPs.\\n\\nFormal pre-registered hypothesis. Let L_primary(T; arm) be the primary endpoint (summed log-PSD error at horizon T; see section 7) computed for arm in {A, B} at T = 4096. The pre-registered alternative is a paired cross-seed comparison at T = 4096:\\n- H0: E[L_primary(4096; B)] = E[L_primary(4096; A)]\\n- H1 (directional, pre-specified): E[L_primary(4096; B)] less than E[L_primary(4096; A)]\\n\\nA two-tailed 95 percent paired-bootstrap CI on L_primary(B) minus L_primary(A) is reported. Rejection of H0 requires the CI to exclude 0 with the directional sign of H1.\\n\\nWhy this is primary: log-domain PSD is the most directly interpretable diagnostic of multi-timescale fidelity; each decade of period contributes a distinct band; the log-domain MSE penalises order-of-magnitude distortions that linear RMSE would dilute. The longest horizon (4096) is where the failure mode -- loss of long-timescale coherence -- should manifest most strongly if it exists, and where the cost gap between attention and Hyena should be visible.\\n\\nWhy the treatment might help: bidirectional neighbourhood context lets the operator resolve local ambiguity using immediate neighbours in a way that strictly causal attention must recover across many steps, where compounding error depresses long-timescale coherence.\\n\\nWhy it might not: the future-window protocol at inference is shared by both arms, so any buffering-related advantage is symmetric; local fidelity may trade off against long-horizon gain; the bottleneck may lie outside the time operator.\\n\\n### 2. Treatment structure\\n\\nTwo primary arms:\\n- Arm A -- SiT-TimeAttn (baseline). SiT with its original time-axis attention. Causality flag is whatever the original SiT specification uses (typically causal-masked); that flag is recorded and held fixed for Arm A. All other components identical to Arm B.\\n- Arm B -- SiT-Hyena (treatment). SiT with the time-axis attention replaced by the Hyena-family operator defined below. Non-causal: bidirectional along the time axis.\\n\\nHyena time operator exact specification:\\n1. Two short 1-D convolutions along the time axis, suggested kernel sizes 3 and 3, producing intermediate features u and v.\\n2. Element-wise multiplicative gate g = sigma(Linear(u)) multiplied element-wise by v, with sigma as a configurable nonlinearity (default GELU).\\n3. Implicit long convolution via FFT: project the gated signal with an implicitly parameterised long kernel, multiply by FFT, apply inverse FFT. Kernel parameterisation follows Hyena-family practice (e.g., two short MLPs parametrising a small set of values that are then interpolated or windowed to length T).\\n4. Non-causal: no future masking; symmetric padding for the short convolutions; the FFT long convolution is naturally non-causal.\\n\\nThese defaults are placeholders to be locked at wiring-sanity time. The design's conclusions hold for any architectural choice within the Hyena family.\\n\\nOptional secondary sub-arm (clearly labelled, not a primary endpoint):\\n- Arm B-prime -- SiT-Hyena-Causal. Same as B with a strictly causal Hyena (asymmetric padding on the short convolutions and a one-sided modification of the FFT long convolution). This arm separates \\\"Hyena vs attention\\\" from \\\"non-causality vs causality\\\" so that any Hyena-arm gain in the primary endpoint can be attributed correctly. It is reported as a clearly secondary analysis and is not pre-registered as a primary endpoint.\\n\\nWhat is not an arm: a causal-attention sub-arm is not required for the question and is left out of the unified design; the omission is recorded in section 10.\\n\\n### 3. Controlled variables (held fixed across both arms)\\n\\nThe following are explicitly held constant between Arm A and Arm B (and Arm B-prime unless flagged).\\n\\nArchitecture outside the time operator: spatial mixing block (operator, depth, width); cross-axis / conditioning pathway including class, text, diffusion-time conditioning as in source; token / patch / positional embeddings including rotary / ALiBi / sinusoidal; normalisation placement and type; output head; number of SiT blocks; embedding dimension; attention / operator width.\\n\\nOptimisation and schedule: optimiser AdamW with beta1 0.9, beta2 0.95, fixed weight decay; learning-rate schedule (peak value, warmup length, decay shape); total training tokens; total optimiser steps; batch size; gradient accumulation; mixed-precision policy; gradient-clipping value; dropout rates; random seeds for data sampling, parameter init, dropout.\\n\\nDiffusion and sampling: noise schedule (cosine / linear / flow-matching parameter, fixed once chosen); sampling procedure with the same solver and the same number of function evaluations (NFE) and step-size schedule; identical inference protocol (chunked two-pass rollout with fixed future-window substitution; see section 4) applied identically across arms.\\n\\nData: preprocessing pipeline (normalisation, channel ordering, clipping); training sequence length held at a single value within the train-short regime; evaluation uses the length ladder; train / validation / test split fixed across arms; held-out ground truth for evaluation (analytic closed-form for the synthetic generator; numerical integration at fine resolution for Lorenz-63).\\n\\nCost control: matched FLOPs at forward-pass level referenced to the training sequence length; parameter count and activation memory (peak bytes per forward pass) reported separately and not part of the cost-control target; wall-clock is not claimed as an endpoint in this design-only deliverable.\\n\\nHardware and software: single accelerator target, one GPU type, to be specified at run time; single software stack (framework version, CUDA, library versions); determinism flags where supported.\\n\\nThe single item explicitly allowed to vary between arms is the time-axis operator. Arm B-prime further varies causal/non-causal masking within the Hyena family.\\n\\n### 4. Inference protocol for non-causality (applied identically across arms)\\n\\nChunked two-pass rollout with fixed future-window substitution rule. At generation time, the model produces chunks of length C (suggested default 128), with a fixed future-window size W (suggested default 64) used to buffer the next W predicted steps. Within each chunk, the time-axis operator sees both past context and the buffered future exactly as in training. After producing each chunk, the window is shifted: the first C produced steps are kept as the roll-out, the buffered W future steps are discarded and replaced by the next chunk's predictions.\\n\\nThe protocol is applied identically in Arms A, B, and B-prime, with the same C and W, the same chunk boundaries, and the same substitution rule. Within a chunk, the Hyena operator runs bidirectionally as designed; the attention baseline performs attention over the same buffered span with the same masking rules as in training. By equating the windowed span between arms, the protocol does not systematically favour one operator.\\n\\n### 5. Datasets and ground truth\\n\\n5.1 Synthetic multi-timescale trajectories (primary benchmark).\\nGenerator: a deterministic continuous-time signal x(t) constructed as a sum of three or more components spanning at least three decades of characteristic period. Canonical form:\\nx(t) = A_s sin(2 pi t / T_s + phi_s) + A_m sin(2 pi t / T_m + phi_m) + x_l(t)\\nwhere T_s = 1, T_m = 100, l corresponds to a slow component with characteristic period T_l at least 10^4. Phases are fixed per generated sample to permit phase-coherence metrics. Realisations are produced at a sampling rate commensurate with the shortest component (at least 10 samples per shortest period) and discretised.\\n\\nGround truth available analytically: closed-form PSD P_x(f) as a sum of delta peaks for sinusoidal components plus an analytic PSD for the slow component; closed-form autocorrelation R_x(tau); closed-form phase structure (instantaneous amplitude and frequency). This is the primary benchmark because every key property in the central question admits an analytic ground truth.\\n\\n5.2 Lorenz-63 (grounding in physical dynamics).\\nGenerator: Lorenz-63 ODE at standard parameters (sigma = 10, rho = 28, beta = 8/3), integrated with a fine-step RK4 (or equivalent) at small step size (e.g. delta t = 0.005 in Lorenz time units), downsampled to the discretisation rate used for evaluation. The (x, y, z) trajectory is treated as a multi-channel signal; PSD and other metrics are computed per channel and averaged.\\nGround truth: held-out fine-resolution numerical integration (Lorenz has no closed-form PSD). The horizon ladder uses integration-time units commensurate with the chosen discretisation rate. Lorenz-63 is included because its multi-timescale structure (fast orbital timescale vs slow switching between lobes) is well-studied and physically interpretable.\\n\\n5.3 Across datasets. Multiple timescales is operationalised as at least three decade-separated period bands (short, medium, long). Extremely long is operationalised as the 4096-step evaluation horizon in section 6, with shorter horizons retained for diagnostic purposes.\\n\\n5.4 Out of scope. Image-video SiT (UCF-101 / Kinetics long-horizon video); long-horizon language / discrete-token trajectories; real-world motion-capture benchmarks. The unified design does not run these; extensions are noted but not part of this design.\\n\\n### 6. Evaluation ladder and protocol\\n\\nSequence lengths: 64 / 256 / 1024 / 4096 steps. Each length is evaluated identically in both arms with the same protocol (section 4) and the same chunk size C and future window W. Training length is held fixed within the train-short regime and reused across arms.\\n\\nFor each (length, seed) pair the protocol produces one generated roll-out, pointwise deterministic forecast skill versus held-out ground truth, spectral estimates and band-decomposed errors, and cost figures at forward-pass level for the chosen length.\\n\\nLength varies as the ladder dimension (64, 256, 1024, 4096). Seed varies as the randomness dimension (at least 3 seeds per arm per length per dataset). The primary endpoint commits to T = 4096; the ladder exists for transparency and reproducibility, including locating any cross-over length at which the relative ordering of A and B reverses.\\n\\n### 7. Endpoints with exact formulas\\n\\nNotation. x-hat = (x-hat[1], ..., x-hat[T]) is a single generated trajectory; x-star = (x-star[1], ..., x-star[T]) is the corresponding ground-truth trajectory. For multi-channel data (Lorenz), endpoints are computed per channel and averaged. Welch PSD with fixed window length W_welch (distinct from the future-window W) and 50 percent overlap is the default estimator; parameters are fixed across arms.\\n\\nPre-registration: the endpoints, the log-PSD convention, the band partition, the forecast horizons, and the set of autocorrelation lags are written down before any data is examined and locked in this design document.\\n\\n7.1 Primary endpoint.\\n\\nL_primary(T = 4096) = sum over bands b in {short, medium, long} of (1 / |B_b|) times sum over f in B_b of (log P-hat(f) - log P-star(f))^2\\n\\nwhere P-hat(f) is the PSD estimator of x-hat, P-star(f) is the ground-truth PSD (analytic for synthetic; estimator of held-out fine integration for Lorenz), and {B_b} is a fixed partition of the frequency axis into three bands aligned to the period decades of the generator (e.g. for synthetic, B_short = {f : f in [f_s/2, 2 f_s]}, B_medium = {f : f in [f_m/2, 2 f_m]}, B_long = {f : f in [1/(2T), f_l/2]}, with f_s, f_m, f_l derived from T_s, T_m, T_l). The log-domain MSE in each band is summed across bands. Lower is better.\\n\\n7.2 Secondary endpoints.\\n\\nForecast RMSE and NLL at multiple horizons. H is a fixed subset of {16, 64, 256, 1024}, evaluated during roll-out (avoiding T itself so the endpoint is on the trajectory, not at the end-point only).\\n\\nRMSE(h) = sqrt( (1 / (N_s N_h)) sum_{s=1..N_s} sum_{t : t+h in [1,T]} (x-hat^{(s)}[t+h] - x-star^{(s)}[t+h])^2 )\\n\\nNLL(h) = -(1 / (N_s N_h)) sum_{s,t} log p_theta_s( x-hat^{(s)}[t+h] | x-hat^{(s)}[<= t] )\\n\\nwith the predictive density p_theta parameterised by the model head at training time; if the head is deterministic, NLL is replaced by a Gaussian-fit residual NLL as a clearly-labelled degradation.\\n\\nAutocorrelation consistency at multiple lags. L-set subset of {1, 8, 64, 512}.\\n\\nAC_tau(x) = sum_{t=1..T-tau} (x[t] - x-bar)(x[t+tau] - x-bar) / sum_{t=1..T} (x[t] - x-bar)^2\\n\\nEndpoint is AC-MSE = mean over tau in L-set of (AC_tau(x-hat) - AC_tau(x-star))^2, per seed, then averaged.\\n\\nWasserstein-1 on increments. Distribution of Delta_t x-hat = x-hat[t+1] - x-hat[t] vs Delta_t x-star. Reported as W_1 using the empirical one-dimensional Wasserstein-1 with linear interpolation. Sensitive to distributional drift; complementary to AC-MSE.\\n\\nTraining stability. Per training run: (i) maximum and final values of the gradient norm; (ii) loss-curve shape (smoothed training loss at fixed iteration milestones); (iii) NaN / Inf event count; (iv) effective rank of the penultimate-block activations on a held-out batch (mean across a small calibration set). Each is a scalar reported per seed.\\n\\nScale of cost curve. Analytical estimate of forward FLOPs as a function of T in {64, 256, 1024, 4096} for each arm, and empirical activation-memory estimate at the training sequence length, reported on the same axes as supporting evidence, not as components of the cost-control target.\\n\\nThe secondary endpoints do not affect acceptance or rejection of H0; they support interpretation of the primary result.\\n\\n7.3 What is intentionally not an endpoint in this design.\\n\\nWall-clock training time (no GPU / no execution; wall-clock not claimed); qualitative perceptual scores for held-out humans (out of scope); any metric on a held-out distinct dataset (e.g. Internet video) -- dataset choice is locked to synthetic plus Lorenz.\\n\\n### 8. Statistical analysis plan\\n\\nPre-registration. The primary endpoint, the directional alternative H1, the seed count, the bootstrap resample count, and the bootstrap CI definition are locked in this design document before any execution. No test is performed in the present sandbox; pre-registration here applies to any future execution of this design.\\n\\nSeeds. Minimum N_seeds = 3 per arm, per dataset, per evaluation length, paired by seed (seed s for Arm A and seed s for Arm B share their data-sampling seed and differ only in operator). Increasing N_seeds is encouraged if budget allows; the minimum is the smallest count that yields a paired bootstrap CI with practical width. The seed count is part of pre-registration.\\n\\nPaired comparison. For each arm, theta in {A, B}, compute ell_s^{(theta)} = L_primary^{(s)}(theta) at T = 4096, where s indexes the seed. The paired difference is d_s = ell_s^{(B)} - ell_s^{(A)}. The summary statistic of interest is d-bar (mean difference).\\n\\nBootstrap CI. Resample seed index with replacement B_boot = 10000 times; for each resample compute d-bar-star. The 95 percent CI is the percentile interval (d-bar-star at 0.025, d-bar-star at 0.975). Reject H0 in favour of the directional H1 iff the entire CI lies below zero. Otherwise, report the CI and the point estimate.\\n\\nMultiplicity. The ladder produces L_primary at four lengths; only T = 4096 is the pre-registered primary. Shorter lengths are reported as the secondary diagnostic L_64, L_256, L_1024 but no multiplicity correction is pre-applied and they are not used for acceptance or rejection.\\n\\nSecondary endpoints. Reported with the same paired-bootstrap structure, sign of effect recorded but no rejection criterion pre-registered. Band-level decomposition of the primary endpoint is reported as a decomposition, not as separate endpoints.\\n\\nEffect-size reporting. Always report point estimate, 95 percent CI, and the matched-FLOPs configuration under which it was obtained. Do not report p-values as the primary inferential quantity; the bootstrap CI is the inferential quantity.\\n\\nMissing-data handling. If a seed's run fails (NaN, divergence), the seed is replaced by another seed up to a maximum of N_seeds + 2 runs per arm; failed runs are recorded and reported.\\n\\n### 9. Feasibility checks\\n\\nWiring-sanity plan (the only execution-grade check that could in principle be performed outside this sandbox). A small smoke run, not a result of the study, would be performed in a real execution environment to confirm that both arms are wired correctly before any production training begins. It is listed as a plan, not a result. The actual check is not performed in this sandbox; this design-only deliverable does not claim any run succeeded.\\n\\nSanity data: a tiny trajectory of length T_train = 256, generated by a single-band sinusoid plus small noise, with analytic ground truth.\\nSanity schedule: very short training (a few hundred optimiser steps), small batch, no scheduling tricks; identical hyperparameters across arms except the time operator.\\nSanity check: after training, the model is asked to denoise a freshly initialised noisy real trajectory and produce a qualitative plot. Expected behaviour: both arms reproduce the sinusoid shape with phase approximately preserved and amplitude close to the true value, up to under-training tolerance. Failure looks like a flat output, a constant mean, a phase-shift inversion, or a divergent curve.\\nWhat this does not establish: wiring-sanity does not establish that the primary endpoint shows a treatment difference, nor that the result generalises beyond the sanity-task distribution.\\nNo run is reported here: this deliverable is the design; no execution is claimed or implied.\\n\\nDesign-side feasibility checks (those that can be performed in the present sandbox, no execution of the experiment):\\n- workspace inspection confirmed empty of SiT/Hyena materials (see evidence)\\n- environment inspection confirmed Python 3.12.3 with 24 CPUs, no GPU, no Torch, no network\\n- pre-registration mechanics: each formula and each pre-registered step (seed count, bootstrap resample count, endpoint definition, ladder, dataset, protocol) is stated in this document and reproducible from sections 6 to 8 alone\\n\\n### 10. Residual risks and conditions that would invalidate the design's conclusions\\n\\nThe conclusions of any future run of this design would be invalidated under any of the following conditions.\\n\\n1. Matched-FLOPs material-change risk. Setting matched FLOPs exactly equal forces width/depth trade-offs that could themselves drive any observed difference. Conclusion invalidated if the recipe silently changes another component. Mitigation: report and constrain depth / width bounds so the recipe cannot reach a degenerate configuration; include a sensitivity over at least one alternative matched-FLOPs recipe.\\n\\n2. Inference-protocol favouritism risk. The chunked two-pass rollout gives the buffered future to both arms. If buffering interacts unexpectedly with one operator but not the other, the protocol could favour one arm non-trivially. Conclusion invalidated if the protocol's effects are not symmetric. Mitigation: verify symmetry by an ablation of buffer size W in {0, 32, 64, 128}; if attention's relative performance differs strongly across W but Hyena's does not, revisit the protocol.\\n\\n3. Synthetic-saturation risk. If both arms reach essentially the analytic ground truth on the synthetic dataset by T = 1024, the experiment may fail to discriminate at T = 4096. Conclusion invalidated only if the failure is silent. Mitigation: report band-decomposed decomposition at all ladder steps; report a noise-robustness stress test (PSNR of generated trajectories under added input noise).\\n\\n4. Lorenz-63 idiosyncrasy risk. The fast/slow separation in Lorenz is specific, so behaviour on it may not generalise to other physical systems. Conclusion invalidated if the design's claim is interpreted too broadly. Mitigation: report band definitions and per-band decomposition; document limits of inference in the discussion.\\n\\n5. PSD-estimator bias risk at long horizon. Welch on T = 4096 with default settings biases the low-frequency band toward higher variance than the analytic formula; bias is unstable across seeds. Conclusion on the long band could be unreliable even if the headline number is statistically significant elsewhere. Mitigation: enforce a fixed Welch configuration across arms, report the band-level L_b values alongside the sum, and require the long-band effect to be consistent in sign across at least two PSD-estimator settings (Welch and Bartlett).\\n\\n6. Low-seed detection-power risk. N_seeds = 3 with bootstrap CIs produces wide intervals; the design may fail to detect a real effect (false negative). Conclusion of non-inferiority would be weakened; conclusion of superiority would be unwarranted. Mitigation: report effect size and CI width explicitly; do not infer \\\"no effect\\\" from an underpowered comparison.\\n\\n7. Optional causal sub-arm risk. Arm B-prime uses a non-standard causal mask on the FFT long convolution. If incorrectly implemented, the sub-arm's interpretation collapses. Conclusion of the secondary causal-vs-non-causal decomposition would be invalidated. Mitigation: Arm B-prime is explicitly labelled secondary; its failure does not invalidate the primary arm comparison.\\n\\n8. Pre-registration scope risk. The pre-registration locks the primary endpoint, the primary horizon, the seed count, and the bootstrap definition. It does not lock corrections for restarts, hyperparameter re-tuning, or non-seed-conditioned learning-rate re-tuning. If post-hoc tuning is allowed, the design's type-I error is uncontrolled. Mitigation: declare a tuning budget (e.g. 10 percent of compute reserved for tuning, not in reported results) at design time.\\n\\n9. Wall-clock absence risk. Because wall-clock is not claimed in this design-only deliverable, claims about practical efficiency are out of scope. Any extension to claim wall-clock benefits would require a separate, environment-specific design. Conclusion invalidated if claimed. Mitigation: report FLOPs and activation memory analytically (no execution); mark any extension as out of scope.\\n\\n10. Concept-of-consistent ambiguity risk. \\\"Consistent\\\" can be read as trajectory-level fidelity, distributional fidelity, or self-consistency across the roll-out (no drift, no artefacts, no mode collapse). The primary endpoint addresses fidelity (point and spectral); distributional self-consistency is addressed only by Wasserstein-1 and marginal moments. Conclusion invalidated if \\\"consistent\\\" is interpreted in a sense not covered by the metrics. Mitigation: report long-run moments of x-hat (drift, variance, kurtosis) and a low-order Wasserstein-1 on the empirical marginal distribution as clearly-labelled supporting evidence.\\n\\n11. Sandbox-execution impossibility risk. No part of training, sampling, evaluation, or wiring-sanity has been executed in the present sandbox; any claim that the design's predictions were confirmed cannot be made from this deliverable. Mitigation: this design document explicitly disclaims execution.\\n\\n### 11. Repository observations (evidence)\\n\\n- /workspace contains only task.json (511 bytes), which restates the question and the note track.md = \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\\\". No SiT, Hyena, or trajectory-modelling references exist locally.\\n- $HOME, /tmp, /data are empty of any relevant material.\\n- No topical directory (SiT / Hyena / paper / repo) is reachable anywhere up to depth 3.\\n- Python 3.12.3 with 24 CPU cores available; no GPU (nvidia-smi cannot communicate with a driver); no Torch installed; network disabled per task spec.\\n- These observations are consistent with task.json and with the design-only nature of the deliverable.\\n\\n### 12. Summary of the locked design in one screen\\n\\nArms: A -- SiT-TimeAttn baseline; B -- SiT-Hyena non-causal treatment. Optional sub-arm: B-prime -- SiT-Hyena-Causal (secondary).\\n\\nDatasets: synthetic multi-timescale (analytic ground truth, at least three decade bands); Lorenz-63 (held-out fine-integration ground truth).\\n\\nSequence elements: continuous-valued.\\n\\nTime axis: roll-out / temporal axis.\\n\\nLength ladder: 64 / 256 / 1024 / 4096, identical across arms.\\n\\nTraining and eval regime: train-short, eval-long.\\n\\nInference protocol: chunked two-pass rollout with fixed future-window substitution; identical across arms.\\n\\nCost control: matched FLOPs; parameter count and activation memory reported separately; wall-clock not claimed.\\n\\nPrimary endpoint: L_primary(T=4096) = sum over bands b of (1/|B_b|) times sum over f in B_b of (log P-hat(f) - log P-star(f))^2 across three decade bands.\\n\\nSecondary endpoints: forecast RMSE and NLL at horizons 16, 64, 256, 1024; AC-MSE at lags 1, 8, 64, 512; Wasserstein-1 on increments; training-stability scalars; FLOPs and activation cost curve.\\n\\nPre-registration: locked at design time -- primary endpoint, primary horizon, seed count, bootstrap count, CI definition.\\n\\nStatistical test: paired bootstrap CI on L_primary(B) - L_primary(A) at T = 4096, 95 percent CI, B_boot = 10000, at least 3 seeds paired by seed.\\n\\nHypotheses: H0 equal; H1 Hyena lower one-sided; CI-excludes-0 criterion.\\n\\nControls (held fixed): spatial mixing, conditioning, embeddings, optimiser, schedule, batch, seed, noise schedule, sampling, NFE, solver, preprocessing, hardware, software, training length.\\n\\nFeasibility check: wiring-sanity on a 256-step single-band sinusoid in a real execution environment (not performed in this sandbox; explicitly disclaimed).\\n\\nOut of scope: image or video SiT, discrete-token trajectories, wall-clock as primary endpoint, real-world motion benchmarks.\\n\\nSandbox execution: none. Deliverable is a design document.\\n\\n### 13. Items explicitly not answered by the design\\n\\n- no empirical result is provided\\n- no code is provided; the workspace is empty and there is no execution environment\\n- no training run is reported or implied\\n- no prediction is made about which arm will satisfy H1\\n- the two supplementary topics setup_4 and setup_5 are unified into one design per researcher instruction i and are not carried as separate designs\\n\\nEnd of design document. Any future execution of this design should treat this document as the pre-registration artefact; deviations (different primary endpoint, different horizon, different seed count, different bootstrap count, different inference protocol, or post-hoc tuning outside a declared tuning budget) should be reported as such and weaken the inference to the extent of the deviation.\", \"evidence\": \"Workspace inspection: /workspace contains only task.json (511 bytes); no SiT, Hyena, or trajectory-modelling code, papers, notes, or references exist locally (verified at both inspection and start of result drafting). No topical directory is reachable anywhere up to depth 3; only irrelevant system paths (e.g. /usr/share/maven-repo) match. $HOME, /tmp, /data are empty of relevant material.\\nEnvironment inspection: Python 3.12.3 with 24 CPU cores available; no GPU (nvidia-smi cannot communicate with a driver); no torch installed; network disabled per task spec.\\nCombined with task.json statement that the task is design-only and that repositories/compute are not required, these observations confirm the deliverable must be a design document only and no execution is performed in the sandbox.\\nLocked design items traced to the researcher's resolutions: time axis = roll-out / temporal axis (resolution a); Hyena variant = standard Hyena-family with FFT long convolution and non-causal instantiation (resolution b); dataset = synthetic multi-timescale plus Lorenz-63 in train-short / eval-long regime (resolution c); length ladder = 64/256/1024/4096 identical across arms (resolution d); inference = chunked two-pass rollout with fixed future-window substitution applied identically (resolution e); matched FLOPs as cost control with params and activation memory reported separately and wall-clock not claimed (resolution f); primary endpoint = summed log-PSD error at T=4096 across three decade bands with secondary endpoints as proposed (resolution g); statistics = minimum 3 seeds paired by seed, paired bootstrap 95 percent CIs with B_boot=10000, pre-registered (resolution h); single unified design with two primary arms and an optional causal-Hyena sub-arm as secondary (resolution i); continuous-valued sequence elements (resolution j); wiring-sanity feasibility plan with explicit caveat (resolution k); controls explicitly enumerated (resolution k).\\nFormulas locked in section 7 of the document: primary endpoint formula, RMSE/NLL at horizons, autocorrelation consistency MSE, Wasserstein-1 on increments, training-stability scalars, FLOPs and memory cost curve.\\nDisclaimer: no experiment is claimed to have been executed.\", \"assumptions\": [\"[FIXED REQUIREMENT] Deliverable is a design document only; no claim of execution is made.\", \"[FIXED REQUIREMENT] SiT time-axis operator is replaced by a non-causal Hyena-family operator; remaining architecture unchanged.\", \"[FIXED REQUIREMENT] Trajectories must be long (operationalised as the 4096-step ladder step) and span multiple timescales (at least three decade bands).\", \"[Locked, resolution a] Time axis = roll-out / temporal axis of the generated trajectory, not diffusion time.\", \"[Locked, resolution b] Hyena operator = standard family: two short convolutions + multiplicative gating + FFT-implicit long convolution, instantiated bidirectionally; specific kernel sizes and gating parameterisations are placeholders to be locked at wiring-sanity time.\", \"[Locked, resolution c] Datasets = synthetic multi-timescale sequences (at least three decades of period, analytic ground truth) plus Lorenz-63 (held-out fine-integration ground truth), in a train-short / eval-long regime.\", \"[Locked, resolution d] Length ladder = 64 / 256 / 1024 / 4096, evaluated identically across arms.\", \"[Locked, resolution e] Inference protocol = chunked two-pass rollout with fixed future-window substitution, applied identically across arms with the same C and W.\", \"[Locked, resolution f] Cost control = matched FLOPs at forward-pass level; parameter count and activation memory reported separately; wall-clock not claimed.\", \"[Locked, resolution g] Primary endpoint = summed log-PSD error at T=4096 across three decade bands; secondary endpoints as in section 7.2.\", \"[Locked, resolution h] Statistical analysis = minimum 3 seeds per arm per dataset, paired by seed, paired bootstrap 95 percent CIs on the primary endpoint, pre-registered before any hypothetical observation; B_boot = 10000.\", \"[Locked, resolution i] setup_4 and setup_5 unified into one design with two primary arms; causal-Hyena sub-arm included as clearly secondary, not primary.\", \"[Locked, resolution j] Sequence elements are continuous-valued; discrete-token Hyena considerations are out of scope.\", \"[Locked, resolution k] Wiring-sanity = tiny 256-step single-band sinusoid smoke run in a real execution environment, listed as a plan only and explicitly not a reported result.\", \"[Design choice] Chunk size C = 128 and future window W = 64 are placeholders to be locked at wiring-sanity time; design conclusions hold as long as both arms use the same C and W.\", \"[Design choice] Welch-window length and 50 percent overlap are fixed across arms; design does not require optimal Welch parameters, only matched parameters.\", \"[Design choice] Sampling procedure (solver plus NFE) and noise schedule held constant across arms; pre-registration does not depend on which solver or NFE is chosen, only that both are matched.\", \"[Design choice] When the model head is deterministic, NLL is reported as a Gaussian-fit residual NLL with a fixed isotropic variance estimate, clearly labelled.\", \"[Risk acknowledged, section 10] Design does not include a held-out distinct benchmark beyond synthetic and Lorenz-63; scope of any claim is limited to those two settings.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"[Unresolvable locally] Any future execution must run in a real execution environment with a GPU; the present sandbox has none. Wiring-sanity in section 9 is a future step only and is not performed here.\", \"[Open] Embedding dimension, block count, attention / operator width are not yet locked; must be chosen with the matched-FLOPs recipe at design finalisation. Design shape holds for any compatible choice.\", \"[Resolved per resolution i] setup_4 and setup_5 unified into one design here; if the researcher later prefers two separate designs, the primary endpoint, dataset, ladder, protocol, and statistical plan above are reusable without change.\", \"[Open, risk in section 10] Whether longer-horizon structural metrics (long-band log-PSD, AC-MSE at lag 512) are stable across PSD-estimator settings; Welch-vs-Bartlett sensitivity is listed as mitigation.\", \"[Open, risk in section 10] Whether minimum seed count (3) yields a bootstrap CI narrow enough to detect the effect size of interest; explicitly documented as a residual risk.\", \"[Open, risk in section 10] Whether the synthetic generator's analytic ground truth is itself a fair test (sufficient phase variety, sufficient slow-component complexity) to expose a Hyena-attention difference.\", \"[Open, risk in section 10] Whether the chunked two-pass rollout protocol is genuinely symmetric between Attention and Hyena, especially around causal-mask conventions inside a buffered span.\", \"[Open, risk in section 10] Whether the matched-FLOPs recipe can be implemented without silently changing another architectural component that drives any observed effect.\", \"[Resolved but noted] Whether original SiT uses causal or non-causal time-axis attention as its baseline; recorded as baseline's actual causality and held fixed in Arm A.\", \"[Resolved but noted] Whether the Hyena FFT-implicit long convolution uses the original Hyena implicit-parametrisation or an equivalent such as Hyena-SE; family level, not specific kernel-size recipe, drives the conclusion.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-95a1a804-b22c-47d5-afcc-ac40b115eb75", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 27}
